    'version_history' => [
        ['version' => '2.68.0', 'date' => '2026-09-10', 'summary' => 'ONE PRODUCT, ONE VOCABULARY -- the language hierarchy applied to the last surfaces still running the policy it replaced. THE DEFECT. IOMS is deliberately bilingual and v2.53.0 set the rule: ENGLISH for module and feature names, navigation, page titles, column headers, status labels and action labels; INDONESIAN for explanatory sentences, help text and guidance. The five department Overview dashboards never got that memo. They had been translated wholesale in v1.11.7 under the older standardize-on-Indonesian policy, and nothing re-checked them when v2.53.0 replaced it. The result was visible on one screen at a time: the sidebar read Overview and the page called itself Ringkasan HSE; the sidebar read CAPA and the card under it read Tindakan Perbaikan; the sidebar read Material Request and the page read Permintaan Material. Clicking Observasi Keselamatan landed on a page titled Safety Observation. The reason v2.53.0 gave for keeping that layer English is exactly this: a user has to be able to say open Regulations and Standards out loud and have it match the screen. WHAT CHANGED, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY DID NOT. Every page title, stat label, card title, module-card name, column header and action link on the HSE, HR, Logistics, Warehouse and Project Management Overviews is now English and matches both the sidebar entry that opens it and the page it navigates to. Every subtitle, card description, empty state and hint on those same pages is still Indonesian, because the policy assigns explanatory copy to Indonesian on purpose. The split is per SLOT, not per page, which is what makes it checkable. THE PUBLIC SITE, FINISHED. The same conversion had been running page by page since v2.56.0 -- acquisition flow, then the landing page in v2.61.0, then its server-supplied steps and FAQ answers in v2.64.0 -- and had stalled with Platform, Solutions and Contact still in Indonesian prose. A visitor going from an English home page to Platform changed language mid-journey, and the FAQ managed three switches inside one page: Indonesian heading, English questions, Indonesian buttons. Those three pages and PublicController DOMAINS are now English, established terms untouched (Permit To Work, HIRADC, JSA, LOTO, PPE, CAPA, RFQ, NCR, FPB, SPK). Legal documents stay Indonesian, deliberately, for an Indonesian legal audience. A DEAD DICTIONARY REMOVED. resources/js/lib/id.js was the terminology map for the superseded policy. It had been imported by nothing for many releases -- its strings did not appear in the compiled bundle at all -- and it is deleted rather than left as something a future reader could wire back up and quietly revert a product decision. LABELS THAT COULD NOT BE READ. Measured at 1440px, the Dashboard KPI strip gave each label 33px: Fatality, the most severe category in an HSE product, rendered as FAT... Two more read PENDING PROCUREM... and MAINTENANCE DUE (... The strip was eight columns wide regardless of viewport; it is now four until 2xl. More importantly the shared StatCard, KpiSummaryCard and ModuleCard no longer truncate their LABEL at all -- a value may be clipped safely because it is a number and the card links to the record, but the label is the only thing saying what the number counts, so it wraps instead. Zero truncated labels on Dashboard and every department Overview at 375, 430, 768, 1024 and 1440. A CHART THAT MEANT SOMETHING IT DID NOT MEAN. Employees by Department was a pie with fourteen slices drawn from a nine-colour categorical palette containing the exact red and green this product uses semantically everywhere else, so a department rendered red read as a department in trouble and colour ran out after nine. It is ranked horizontal bars in one brand colour now, top ten with the remainder rolled into a muted Other, which answers the ranking question the card is actually asked. Separately, each KPI category already carries an admin-configured colour that its card and sparkline use; the trend chart ignored it and coloured by position, so Fatality was red on its card and blue on the line beneath it. Both payloads already carry the category code, so they are joined in the page rather than in DashboardStatsService: presentation, not query. THE ERROR PAGE WAS UNREACHABLE FOR THE CASE THAT NEEDS IT. Errors/Show was built in v1.11.3.2 so an expected error would render inside the product with a way back. It only ever rendered for requests carrying the X-Inertia header -- navigation started from inside the already-running app. Every full-page request fell through to a bare 404 NOT FOUND with no navigation: a stale bookmark, a link pasted from a chat, a refresh after the session expired, a deep link into a department the user cannot open. All of them now get the real page. Reaching more requests meant the message shown reaches more readers, so what gets shown became deliberate: a new ErrorMessagePresenter suppresses framework text that names internals -- No query results for model App Models Employee 99999 was being rendered as the entire explanation of a 404 -- while the three 403 messages this codebase writes on purpose still reach the reader. VERIFIED IN A RUNNING BROWSER against MySQL with real tenant data, which is how most of this was found: the label truncation, the fourteen-slice pie and the bare 404 are all invisible to source reading. Zero horizontal overflow at 320, 375, 430, 768 and 1440 on public and authenticated pages. Two labels still clip at 320px only, where the word OBSERVATIONS is itself wider than its column; recorded rather than papered over. Tests: 315 -> 325 (1497 assertions), including a language-hierarchy test that reads BOTH the JSX label positions and the rendered server props, because all three previous attempts to hold this line were scoped to the file their author happened to be editing and passed while the page said something else. ESLint 0 errors, build clean. No change to tenant isolation, RBAC, entitlements, authentication, subscription, payment, approval rules, PTW rules, workflow transitions or any server-side validation. The error-handler change alters RENDERING only: status codes, the abort and abort_unless calls that produce them, and the production-only handling of 500/503 are untouched.'],
        ['version' => '2.67.0', 'date' => '2026-09-09', 'summary' => 'THE APPLICATION SHELL -- the frame every page is drawn inside, which had quietly become the least accessible part of the product. WHY THIS AND NOT MORE FORMS. v2.65.0 and v2.66.0 made ten forms announce their required fields, associate their errors and keep their actions reachable. The shell wrapping every one of those forms announced its primary navigation as an unlabelled "button", and its mobile drawer was not a dialog. The most accessible part of IOMS was sitting inside the least accessible part. THE OVERFLOW, MEASURED AT LAST. Every authenticated page scrolled sideways in a wide band of viewport sizes. It had been recorded as a known TopBar issue in three consecutive releases and deferred each time as a navigation-phase concern, but it had never actually been measured. Measured against the real compiled stylesheet it is worse than the note claimed: the band starts at 640px, not 768px -- exactly where the search field appears -- and the header overflows by 265px at 640px (41 percent of the viewport), 137px at 768px (iPad portrait), 89px at 1024px (iPad landscape, where the 240px rail eats the gain) and 13px at 1100px. THE CAUSE was a single hard w-[380px] on the global search input. A flex item cannot shrink below an explicit width without min-w-0, so the field never gave way. The reason it survived three releases undiagnosed is subtler and worth recording: flexbox default shrink of 1 let EVERY OTHER control give way a little instead, so the symptom read as "the header looks cramped" rather than "the header is 137 pixels wider than the tablet it is on". THE FIX IS NOT A SMALLER NUMBER. One fixed width cannot serve a header whose contents change at four breakpoints, so the field now has two presentations over one piece of state: nothing below sm (unchanged -- the bottom nav owns navigation there), an icon that opens the field full-size from sm to lg, and the inline field at lg and above where w-[380px] is a basis it may shrink FROM. Every other control is now explicitly shrink-0 and the department selector -- the only unbounded string in the bar, since customers name their own departments -- truncates. With exactly one flexible item the outcome is predictable at every width instead of everything squashing at once. Re-measured: zero overflow at 320, 375, 414, 640, 768, 820, 900, 1024, 1100, 1180, 1280, 1440 and 1920, with the longest realistic department name; the search field grows from 145px at 1024 to its full 380px, and the Ctrl K hint returns at xl where there is room for it. ALSO CORRECTED, AND IT IS THE SAME MISTAKE TWICE. v2.38.0 hid the header Dashboard link below sm because MobileBottomNav pins Dashboard as its first tab -- right reasoning, wrong breakpoint. The bottom nav renders up to lg, not up to sm, so Dashboard was drawn twice on every screen from 640px to 1023px: precisely the band with no room to spare. It now hides to lg, where the duplicate actually stops. THE MOBILE DRAWER WAS NOT A DIALOG. It looked like one -- a scrim and a close button -- and behaved like ordinary page content. Tab walked straight out of it into the page underneath, which was still there and still focusable, so a keyboard user ended up operating a page they could not see through a scrim. Escape did nothing. Closing it dropped focus to <body>, so the next Tab restarted from the top of the document instead of from the control that opened it. Worse, a CLOSED drawer is moved off-screen with a transform, which means it was still rendered and still in the tab order -- roughly twenty invisible links between the header and the page content on every phone. Two small hooks fix this: useFocusTrap (focus enters, Tab and Shift+Tab cycle, Escape closes, focus returns to the opener -- and never to a node a navigation has since detached) and useMediaQuery, which exists because this is the one thing in a responsive shell CSS cannot express: the rail is a permanent landmark above lg and a modal dialog below it, and role is an attribute, not a style. The closed drawer is now inert. A DEFECT IN THE MARKUP ITSELF. The drawer close control was a <span onClick> nested INSIDE the About button. Interactive content inside a button is invalid, and the practical cost was that Close could not be reached or activated from a keyboard at all -- on a phone, where it is the only way to dismiss the drawer short of finding the scrim. They are siblings now and Close is a real button with a name. THE REST OF THE ACCESSIBILITY WORK. A skip link, because without one reaching page content meant tabbing the entire rail and header on EVERY navigation. Accessible names on every icon-only control -- these carried title, which is a tooltip: unreliable to assistive technology and invisible on touch, so they announced as "button". Names on all four landmarks, since three of them are navigation. aria-current on the active sidebar item, the active sub-item and the active bottom-nav tab, whose active state was carried entirely by weight, colour and an indicator bar -- v2.29.0 fixed "easy to miss at a glance" for sighted users and left the same gap for everybody else. The scrim is hidden from assistive technology, because it is decoration. HONEST ABOUT VERIFICATION. The layout is browser-measured against the real compiled stylesheet at thirteen widths, before and after. The keyboard and screen-reader behaviours are covered by contract tests over the shipped source and by review, NOT by interaction in a browser: signing in could not be automated in this environment, and the shell is React that never runs server-side, so an Inertia response is a prop bag with none of this markup in it. That limit is the same one FormExperienceSystemTest records, and it is stated here rather than glossed. Tests: 299 -> 315 (1467 assertions). ESLint 0 errors, build clean. No change to tenant isolation, RBAC, entitlements, authentication, subscription, payment, approval rules, PTW rules or any server-side validation. Navigation VISIBILITY is untouched -- the sidebar is still built from getGlobalNavItems and getSelectableDepartments, gated by role and enabled modules, and a test pins that too. This release changes how the shell behaves and how it reads, never what anyone may reach.'],
        ['version' => '2.66.0', 'date' => '2026-09-09', 'summary' => 'MASTER DATA FORM EXPERIENCE ROLLOUT -- phase 2, and three things the rollout taught that phase 1 could not. UPGRADED: Vendors, Projects, Contractors and Visitors (dedicated page forms), plus PPE Types and Settings > Departments (dialog editors). Six forms, chosen because they are the dedicated master-data surfaces; the twenty workflow forms and the remaining Settings tabs are deliberately untouched and the reasons are recorded in docs/UX_ARCHITECTURE_DISCOVERY.md section 20. THE SYSTEM CHANGED ONCE, AND IT WAS A SUBTRACTION. The rollout hit a real limit: FormActions and DialogFooter both want to be the action area, and an ErrorSummary above five fields already entirely on screen restates what the reader can see. The tempting answer was a second, dialog-flavoured set of components. The right one was to use LESS of the system: page forms take FormSection plus FormField plus FormActions plus ErrorSummary, dialog forms take FormField only. No new component, and a test pins the rule so a dialog cannot quietly grow a second action bar. OWNERSHIP BELONGS NEAR THE TOP -- a new principle, and the clearest finding of the pass. Three of the four page forms buried the Operating Unit field near the bottom; Vendors had it second-to-last, filed under a heading reading "Capability and Notes". That field decides who owns the record and therefore who will ever see it again. Master data exists so the rest of IOMS can rely on it, so the reader has to know whose register they are writing into BEFORE they fill it in. Ownership now sits in the first section beside identity on every converted form. NOT EVERY FORM NEEDS RESTRUCTURING -- the second principle, and the one that keeps this from becoming churn. Projects is seven fields and had no hierarchy problem; inventing sections for it would have added ceremony without clarity. It got the CONTRACT -- accurate required marking, findable errors, a reachable action bar, unsaved-change protection -- and almost nothing else. The contract is the part that applies everywhere; the restructuring is situational. A TERMINOLOGY DEFECT FIXED. The Contractor form labelled its Operating Unit field "Company", directly above a field called "Company Name" meaning the contractor own firm -- two adjacent controls saying Company and meaning opposite things. IOMS renamed Company to Operating Unit for users in v2.54.0 precisely to stop it being read as a separate legal entity; this page had been missed. THE DIRECTORY DROPDOWN APPEARED A NINTH TIME. EmployeeSelector was built in v2.38.0 for exactly this and named seven offending controllers; v2.65.0 found an eighth in Assets and this pass a ninth -- Visitor registration, which shipped the entire employee directory to render a host dropdown. That happens at a gatehouse, on a phone, with somebody waiting, which is the worst possible version of scrolling two thousand names. It searches on demand now and the controller no longer preloads the directory. Server-side validation of the submitted id against the tenant own employees is unchanged. ALSO CORRECTED, from reading the actual FormRequests: Projects marks only company_id, name and status required, since the dates are nullable, and the end_date after_or_equal rule is now stated in a hint rather than discovered on rejection; Vendors marks three of twenty-three fields; Visitors three of eight. Description on the Project form was a single-line Input for a field the server allows 2000 characters in, and is now a Textarea. VERIFIED: sections, required markers, aria-required and screen-reader text all render and match the server rules on the converted pages; the Visitor form ships no employees prop and its host picker searches; actions are sticky at 375px and static from 768px. The 768-1100px horizontal overflow was re-confirmed as the known pre-existing TopBar issue and NOT a regression -- measured on the converted pages and on untouched /dashboard at the same widths, with the offending element outside the form in every case. Tests: 297 -> 299 (1408 assertions). ESLint 0 errors, build clean. No change to tenant isolation, RBAC, entitlements, authentication, payment, approval rules or any server-side validation rule.'],
        ['version' => '2.65.0', 'date' => '2026-09-09', 'summary' => 'THE IOMS FORM EXPERIENCE SYSTEM -- phase 1 of the UX architecture work, and four real defects the conversion uncovered. WHAT THE AUDIT MEASURED, ACROSS ALL 27 MODULE FORMS: required-field marking 0/27, validation error summary 0/27, sticky or otherwise reachable save 0/27, unsaved-change protection 0/27, searchable selector 2/27 with the other 25 using a raw <select>. Meanwhile the PUBLIC signup form already had a Field component that marked required fields, rendered hints and showed errors with an icon -- the prospect filling in a marketing form was better served than the customer using the product. That component is now promoted into Components/shared/form/ where both sides can reach it, with the accessibility wiring it never had. FIVE COMPONENTS AND ONE HOOK, deliberately not a framework. FormSection groups fields under a title AND a one-line description written as a question, which is the transferable principle from PTW -- it is NOT a card by default, because when every group is a card the card stops meaning anything. FormField makes requiredness visible AND programmatic (aria-required, plus a screen-reader text equivalent, because an asterisk is a glyph a screen reader does not read as "required"), wires aria-invalid and aria-describedby, and carries the data-field anchor. ErrorSummary renders on failed submit, takes focus (so the viewport moves AND assistive technology announces it), and each entry is a button that scrolls to and focuses its field. FormActions sticks to the bottom on mobile and goes static from sm, respects safe-area-inset-bottom, and gives destructive actions a separated slot rather than placing Delete next to Save and relying on colour. useUnsavedChanges guards both exits -- Inertia navigation and beforeunload -- and only when the form is genuinely dirty, because a confirmation on an untouched form is how people learn to dismiss confirmations without reading them. A CORRECTION TO THE DISCOVERY DOCUMENT, made while implementing. It recommended adopting Combobox wherever an option list exceeds ~15. Reading Combobox properly showed that was wrong: it operates on STRINGS, allows free text, and returns whatever was typed -- correct for "suggest a department name but let me type a new one", and unsafe for any foreign key, because InCurrentTenant validation and every report that joins on that FK depend on the value being a real id. EmployeeSelector is the id-based answer but is employee-specific and async by design. The gap between them -- an id-backed list already in the page payload -- is the common case and had no primitive at all, so SearchableSelect was built for it: keyboard-operable (arrows, Home/End, Enter, Escape), role=listbox with aria-activedescendant, and a filter box that only appears once the list is long enough to need one. FOUR REAL DEFECTS FOUND WHILE CONVERTING. Assets/Form could not display a validation error on THIRTEEN OF ITS FIFTEEN FIELDS -- only name and company_id had the {errors.x && ...} line, so a server rejection on serial number, purchase date, vendor or attachment produced a form that silently refused to save with nothing on screen. Settings BrandingTab never destructured `errors` from useForm at all, so none of its seventeen fields could show one. Employees/Form rendered its entire action block TWICE, once for the normal case and again inside the intern branch, so a change to one had to be remembered in the other. And writing the alignment test caught my own mistake: the first draft marked position_id, join_date and category as required when all three are nullable server-side -- a form demanding more than the server does is not a safe error, it blocks work the system would have accepted. The test now asserts the marking matches the actual rules. FOUR FORMS CONVERTED, chosen to cover the taxonomy rather than to maximise count: Employees (complex master data, conditional fields, cascading selectors), Assets (simple master data, large selector), Material Requests (workflow, line items, two outcomes), Settings Branding (configuration). Material Requests also needed its item rows rethought for a phone -- a twelve-column grid of seven inputs collapsed into thirty-five anonymous fields with nothing marking where one item ended and the next began; each item is now a numbered card, which is exactly when a card IS the right container. It is also the one form with a legitimate second action, since Draft and Submitted are genuinely different states, and the action bar now states which one Submit produces. NOT CONVERTED, DELIBERATELY: the remaining 23 forms. The abstractions are only four forms old; rolling them across everything now would multiply any mistake in them by 23 before the design has been used in anger. The rollout plan is in docs/UX_ARCHITECTURE_DISCOVERY.md. ALSO REMOVED: the Asset create page no longer preloads the entire employee directory, because its responsible-employee picker now queries on demand -- that page was one of the seven EmployeeSelector was introduced to fix in v2.38.0, and leaving the payload would mean paying for a control that no longer reads it. RELEASE DATE CORRECTED. The discovery found every declared release date running 18-20 days ahead of the actual commit date, because the field is hand-written and each release incremented the previous entry rather than reading the clock. v2.65.0 declares the real date. Earlier entries are left as the historical record they are. A PRE-EXISTING DEFECT FOUND AND NOT FIXED HERE: every authenticated page overflows horizontally between roughly 768px and 1100px -- confirmed on Dashboard, the Employees list and Calendar, none of which this release touches. The cause is the TopBar global search at a fixed w-[380px] plus the notification controls. That is navigation chrome, which this work package was explicitly told not to touch and which the navigation phase will restructure anyway. VERIFIED IN THE BROWSER: sections, descriptions and eight required markers render with matching aria-required and screen-reader text; five searchable selects open on ArrowDown, expose aria-activedescendant, select on Enter and hide their filter box when the list is short; the Operating Unit -> Department -> Position cascade enables and re-hints correctly through the keyboard alone; the unsaved-changes guard fires on a dirty form, blocks navigation when declined, preserves the typed value, and stays silent on a clean form; actions are sticky at 375px and static from 768px; one column at 375px and two above. Tests: 284 -> 297 (1341 assertions). ESLint 0 errors, build clean. No change to tenant isolation, RBAC, entitlements, authentication, payment, PTW rules, approval rules or any server-side validation rule.'],
        ['version' => '2.64.0', 'date' => '2026-09-29', 'summary' => 'LANDING PAGE: THE OPERATING LOOP, plus two defects the redesign uncovered on the way. THE SECTION. "From master data to management reporting" was five bordered cards in a `lg:grid-cols-4` grid -- which, for FIVE steps, orphaned the last card on a second row on every large screen. The shape was also wrong for the argument: five equal boxes say "here are five features", when the point is that ONE record travels all five and comes out the other end as management reporting. It is now five stations on a single rail, so the eye reads a route instead of a list, and after stage 05 the rail curves back to 01 -- because IOMS is a loop, not a funnel, and "what the reporting shows is what the next cycle starts from" is the actual product claim. The return arc is the idea the old strip could not express at all. MOTION THAT MEANS SOMETHING, AND NOTHING THAT DOES NOT. Each station lights as it enters the viewport and the rail fills to match, so scrolling the section IS travelling the route; a small lit head rides the end of the fill, advancing 01 -> 05 as the reader scrolls and perfectly still while they read. Hovering or focusing a station brightens it and quiets its neighbours. A looping dash on the existing `animate-dataflow` keyframe was built first and REMOVED for two reasons: it rendered wrong (vectorEffect="non-scaling-stroke" resolves strokeDasharray in pixels rather than normalised pathLength units, so one dash became fourteen across an 870px rail), and it was redundant next to the fill -- decoration on top of meaning, which is the exact failure this redesign exists to correct. Nothing in the section now animates on a timer. A DEADMAN SWITCH, and the reason it is there. `Reveal` can afford a silent IntersectionObserver because it only ever ADDS a keyframe -- content is visible either way. This component is different in a way that is easy to miss: illumination drives COLOUR, so a station never reported would stay pale forever and the section would look broken rather than un-animated. Found during verification on a page whose animation clock had frozen (a CSSTransition stuck in playState "running", every existing Reveal on the page reporting zero). An observer always delivers an initial callback, so if nothing arrives shortly after mount the mechanism is not running and the honest resting state is fully lit. Costs the choreography, never the design. BACKGROUND. The page had exactly two surfaces: navy bands and plain white, and this section sat on a flat brand-50 wash that read as empty. Making it a fourth navy band was the easy answer and the wrong one -- four dark sections in one scroll flattens the rhythm they exist to create. BlueprintBackdrop gained a `light` variant instead: the same material lit from the other side, with the technical grid drawn in navy at low alpha, two soft blooms in steel and cool indigo well below the content contrast, and a single hairline horizon. A third surface type, so the page alternates rather than repeats. No new component, no image asset, no canvas. MOBILE IS RETHOUGHT, NOT SHRUNK. Five cards at 375px would be five illegible slivers, so the rail turns vertical: a spine down the left with the stations stacked against it, which is the same journey read the way a phone is read. Verified at 375, 768, 1024, 1440 and 1920 with no horizontal overflow at any of them. TWO DEFECTS FOUND ON THE WAY, both the same blind spot. The five stage bodies were still INDONESIAN on an otherwise-English page: v2.61.0 fixed the two blocks that live in Welcome.jsx and missed this one because it arrives as a server prop -- and the test written to pin that fix read the JSX FILE, so it passed for three releases without once looking at the copy. Rewritten to read the rendered Inertia props instead, it immediately found the second: the landing FAQ was Indonesian too, AND still described THREE plans four releases after the four-tier model shipped -- naming Professional as adding "Management visibility", never mentioning Business, and quoting seat capacities for a catalogue that no longer existed. A public FAQ contradicting the pricing table two sections above it is a product-fidelity defect, not a translation one. Both are now English and state the ladder the way PricingService derives it. The `/how-it-works` page shares the same server list, so it was translated with it, and each stage now carries BOTH a short `summary` (the landing rail) and the full `body` (the detail page) from one array -- so the short version can never drift into claiming something the long one does not. Tests: 282 -> 284 (1250 assertions), including one that fails if any server-supplied landing copy reverts to Indonesian and one that pins both lengths on all five stages. No backend logic, pricing, entitlement, tenancy, payment or schema change.'],
        ['version' => '2.63.0', 'date' => '2026-09-28', 'summary' => 'SECURITY FOLLOW-UP: THE BLIND SPOT v2.62.0 LEFT, FOUND BY AUDITING ITS OWN COVERAGE TEST. v2.62.0 made ownership structural for every table carrying a company_id and asserted it with a test that walked MODELS CARRYING A company_id. The tables that have none were never examined, so the test was green while three of them leaked. A table without a company_id is not unowned; it is owned one join away. THREE LIVE BYPASSES, all reproduced against the real database before anything was changed. KPI_RECORDS is owned through its employee, so it received no scope, and KpiInputController::create() shipped KpiRecord::latest()->limit(10)->get() -- the ten most recent KPI records IN THE INSTALLATION. 426 foreign rows were readable as the new tenant, carrying incident dates, quantities and free-text remarks; the v2.62.0 fix already blanked the employee and department names because those relations resolve through scoped models, which is why this survived the first pass looking harmless. DAILY_REPORTS is owned through its project: DailyReportPolicy checked only the role capability canManageDailyReports() and show() had no guard at all, so GET /daily-reports/{id} returned any tenant\'s report and PUT could modify it -- a cross-tenant WRITE. APPROVALS has no owner column and is route-bound by id, and ApprovalEngine::authorize() asked only "does this person hold the deciding role", never "whose record is this", so a Super Admin of one tenant could approve or reject another tenant\'s material request, purchase order or permit. THE FIX REUSES THE EXISTING ARCHITECTURE rather than adding a third mechanism. BelongsToCompanyThrough lets a model name the relation it is owned through and adds whereHas on it, so the parent\'s own CompanyOwnedScope decides what "exists" means and no tenant rule is restated -- the shape EmployeePpe already used by hand, generalised. Applied to 26 models: KPI records, daily reports and their activities and photos, milestones and project activities/manpower/timeline, employee competencies/internships/rosters/shift assignments, every document line item, and contractor/vendor/waste/inspection attachments. Goods receipts get an explicit OR-shaped scope instead, because a receipt can arrive against a purchase order, a material request or straight into a warehouse and any of the three may be null. Approvals are fixed at the authorization layer: an approval whose polymorphic subject does not resolve under the current tenant cannot be decided. SOFT DELETION IS NOT AN OWNERSHIP QUESTION, and getting that wrong would have been worse than the bug. A plain whereHas(\'employee\') also hides the records of every soft-deleted employee, silently changing historical HSE totals as a side effect of a security fix. The trait applies withTrashed() to the parent so only CompanyOwnedScope is doing the isolation, and a test pins it: a departed employee\'s KPI history stays visible to its own tenant and to nobody else. THE RAW QUERY BUILDER AUDIT THIS RELEASE WAS ACTUALLY FOR came back clean. Three DB::table() call sites exist in the whole application, all inside App\\Rules\\InCurrentTenant -- the validation rule whose job is to read a row\'s owner without a scope and compare it against Company::query(), which IS scoped. It fails closed on a null owner. The 26 raw SQL fragments are all selectRaw/orderByRaw/whereRaw appended to an Eloquent builder: projections and sorts, not query entry points, so the global scope still applies. No repository layer, no unguarded DB::select, no UNION, no raw SQL taking user input into a predicate. PPE_TYPES IS DELIBERATELY SHARED and stays that way: six generic rows with a replacement interval, no column identifying a customer. Two consequences are now written down instead of implied. First, a relation from a shared table INTO a scoped one silently becomes a per-tenant question -- PpeType::assignments() returns scoped EmployeePpe rows, so the delete guard had started asking "is MY tenant using this type" and would have let one customer delete a type another was actively issuing. It now asks the installation-wide question explicitly. Second, mutation sits behind role:super_admin, which every customer has one of, so any customer can rename a type or change an interval for everyone -- a PRODUCT question, flagged rather than changed here, because narrowing it would remove a capability customers currently have. REGRESSION COVERAGE: 11 new tests, 9 of which fail with the trait disabled and the other 2 with the approval check removed -- verified by removing each. Both directions on every case. The coverage test now inspects EVERY model and allows an unscoped one only when it is named in an explicit global list with a reason, which is the property the v2.62.0 version was supposed to have and did not. Tests: 271 -> 282 (1197 assertions). Verified live in both tenants: the new tenant\'s KPI Input page went from ten foreign records to zero, while the established tenant still sees its own ten, all 426 of its KPI records and all 60 employees across 18 departments on Reports. No migration, no schema change, no data created, moved or deleted, and no frontend change.'],
        ['version' => '2.62.0', 'date' => '2026-09-28', 'summary' => 'SECURITY: TENANT DATA ISOLATION IS NOW STRUCTURAL, NOT CONVENTIONAL. THE INCIDENT. A newly provisioned Starter tenant opened Reports and saw an existing customer\'s departments and employee names. Reproduced against the real database before anything was changed: acting as the new tenant, the Reports page returned 29 departments of which 25 belonged to another customer, the employee list returned 68 rows of which 60 were another customer\'s, and Employee::find() / Department::find() reached foreign records outright. IT WAS READ LEAKAGE, NOT DATA DUPLICATION, and the distinction was established by forensics rather than assumed: every employee, department and position row in the database was already owned by the company that created it, the new tenant\'s own company held none of them, and TenantProvisioningService creates a fresh Tenant, Company, Subscription and Super Admin with no copy, no clone and no "first existing company" fallback anywhere in it. NO DATA REPAIR WAS REQUIRED and none was performed. Row counts before and after the fix are identical. ROOT CAUSE. Milestone 2 put a global scope on Company and recorded the reasoning in ARCHITECTURE: everything beneath a Company is "safe transitively, because it can only ever reference a Company this scope already filtered". Every clause of that is true about the DATA and none of it is true about a QUERY. A row\'s company_id does point at exactly one Company; nothing forced a query to go looking at Company at all. Transitive safety only held for queries that voluntarily started from Company::query()->pluck(\'id\'), which made isolation a convention spread across 66 models and several hundred call sites. A static sweep found 72 sites that did not follow it. The one in the screenshot is an idiom rather than a typo, which is why it spread: Department::where(\'is_active\', true)->when($companyId, fn ($q) => $q->where(\'company_id\', $companyId)) -- with no company chosen, which is the default, when() is a no-op and every department in the installation comes back. THE FIX IS ONE SCOPE AND ONE TRAIT, mirroring the arrangement Company already had. BelongsToCompany + CompanyOwnedScope are applied to all 63 models carrying a company_id; a company-owned query can now only return rows whose company is one of Company::query()->select(\'id\'), so TenantScope and CompanyAuthorizationScope still decide the answer and this adds no policy of its own -- it makes the existing policy impossible to forget. A subquery rather than a pluck, so a scope on 63 models is not 63 extra round-trips. users is tenant-owned instead (its company_id is legitimately null for a tenant\'s own administrators) and gets UserTenantScope on tenant_id -- three assignee pickers had been listing every user of every customer. employee_ppe and ppe_replacement_request_items have no company_id at all and resolve ownership through their parent. Null owners are unreachable by default, because a row nobody owns must not become a row everybody can see; the three tables where null genuinely means "the built-in default everyone starts from" (KPI categories, numbering formats, numbering sequences) opt in explicitly, and the two owned through another column (activity_logs via its actor, tasks via creator or assignee) say so in their own scope. A SECOND, OLDER DEFECT SURFACED WHILE FIXING THE FIRST. bootstrap/app.php carried the comment "ResolveTenant runs FIRST" for four milestones. It did not: web(append:) puts middleware at the END of the group, after Laravel\'s own SubstituteBindings, so every implicit route-model binding in the application resolved its record with NO tenant in context. That was invisible while nothing beneath Company was scoped -- an unscoped binding finds the row and the controller then rejects a foreign one -- and became a 404 on the user\'s OWN records the moment the scope existed. SubstituteBindings is now removed from the group and re-added after ResolveTenant; the order StartSession -> ResolveTenant -> SubstituteBindings is asserted by a test, and the tinker one-liner for printing the real order is in CONVENTIONS, because the comment had been wrong for a year. VERIFIED IN THE BROWSER AGAINST THE REAL DATABASE, both directions. As the new tenant: Reports lists 4 departments and 1 Operating Unit, all its own; the employee list is its own 8; /employees/3 returns 404; and ?company_id=1 -- naming the other customer\'s Operating Unit explicitly -- returns zero departments rather than theirs. As the established tenant: 25 departments across both its Operating Units, 60 employees, dashboard totals unchanged. Nothing was over-blocked in the course of closing the leak, which is the failure mode on the other side of this fix and is asserted by three of the new tests. REGRESSION COVERAGE: 18 new tests, and 17 of them fail with the scope removed -- verified by removing it. They cover the incident\'s own shape (an established tenant with data, a new one without): each tenant sees its own employees, departments and PPE; the new tenant sees none of the other\'s through lists, search, pagination, reports, direct id access or a tampered company_id parameter; provisioning produces a tenant owning only its own rows; the established tenant\'s data is unchanged. One test walks every model with a company_id and asserts it carries an isolation scope, so the next table cannot be added without one by accident. Two pre-existing tests were updated rather than the code: a count asserting a DATABASE fact now says withoutGlobalScopes(), and a cross-tenant update went from 403 to 404 because binding no longer resolves the foreign record -- the stronger answer, since 403 confirms a record exists to somebody with no right to know. Tests: 253 -> 271 (1175 assertions). Seeders re-verified idempotent on a scratch database. No migration, no schema change, and no data was created, moved or deleted. Nothing was solved by hiding rows in the frontend. No pricing, subscription, Midtrans, email, entitlement or provisioning behaviour was changed.'],
        ['version' => '2.61.0', 'date' => '2026-09-27', 'summary' => 'THE PUBLIC SITE, REDESIGNED AROUND WHAT THE PRODUCT ACTUALLY IS -- and three places where the marketing copy named a workspace the product does not have. THE PRICING LADDER NOW READS AS A LADDER. Business grants five departments and the card listed all five flat, which read as "Business is five new things" when two of them are Starter\'s and Professional\'s. PricingService::withLadderScope() derives each tier against the one below it from the SAME config/plans.php scope the entitlement layer uses, so every public surface now says "Everything in Professional, plus Project Management, Logistics / PPIC and Procurement" -- three operational domains, which is what the tier actually is -- and it cannot drift from what a customer is granted because it is not copy, it is a diff. Enterprise ends on "Every department IOMS ships" rather than reciting a list. Prices, entitlements, checkout, webhook and provisioning are untouched. THREE TERMINOLOGY ERRORS, ALL THE SAME KIND. The product showcase carried a WAREHOUSE tab displaying item master, stock levels and goods receipt -- every one of which belongs to LOGISTICS / PPIC. `warehouse` in the workspace registry is a Dashboard and an Overview and nothing else; the tab was showing a real capability under a workspace name that does not own it, which is the one inaccuracy a product showcase cannot afford. The platform grid carried an OPERATIONS card that was not a workspace at all, just a bucket holding Work Center, Tasks, Man-Hour and the activity timeline -- the CROSS-CUTTING layer every department shares, now presented as a layer instead of a ninth domain a customer would go looking for. And PROCUREMENT & WAREHOUSE merged two things the product separates. config/plans.php gained a `shells` key naming warehouse and finance so PricingService drops them from a tier\'s "plus" list: they are still GRANTED, they are simply never sold. THE HERO DIAGRAM MOVES, AND THE MOTION IS THE ARGUMENT. Eight domains on a circle around an IOMS hub was the right idea rendered as a still diagram -- eight boxes and eight hairlines, identical on first paint and on the hundredth second, read as a logo arrangement rather than a claim about the software. Each spoke now carries one dashed segment travelling from its department INTO the hub, staggered so arrivals never line up into a heartbeat, with one slow ring leaving the centre as they land. That is the product claim stated literally: departments produce records, the records land in IOMS, management reads one set of data. Every node is a real button -- hover or focus highlights its spoke and names what that department records (Permit To Work, item master, purchase requisition), so the diagram answers a question instead of decorating a section. THE PROBLEM SECTION SHOWS THE CHANGE INSTEAD OF ASSERTING IT. Six identical grey boxes, a rotated arrow and a bordered box reading "One connected operation" stated the conclusion; the six boxes were interchangeable rectangles when the whole point is that today\'s tools are six DIFFERENT places. It is now two panels: TODAY, six tools each with its own icon and the department that owns it, on a hatched surface; and IN IOMS, ONE record -- MR-2026-00126 raised in Logistics / PPIC, charged to a project, ordered by Procurement as PO-2026-00042, received against GRN-2026-00097, visible in Report Center -- with the statuses IOMS actually stores. Every step is a route that exists. A converging bracket physically narrows the six lines into one. THE DEPARTMENT GRID IS EIGHT DOMAINS, NOT SIX WHITE CARDS. Maintenance, Asset Management and Quality Control shipped and were missing from the section entirely. Each card now carries one accent used in three quiet places -- a filled gradient icon chip (the application\'s own stat-card chip), a hairline top rule, and a wash that appears only on hover -- with a lift and a sliding arrow. Restraint is deliberate: eight cards each shouting a different colour is the failure mode on the other side of six identical white boxes. THE SHOWCASE IS SIX WORKSPACES AND EVERY LABEL IS THE REAL ONE. Dashboard, HSE, Human Resources, Project Management, Logistics / PPIC and Procurement -- which is also exactly the plan ladder, so a visitor comparing tiers can see what each one adds. Human Resources and Project Management, two of the four departments the ladder is built on, had not appeared anywhere. The stat labels are not invented KPI names: they are what Pages/Hse/Dashboard.jsx, Pages/Hr/Dashboard.jsx, Pages/ProjectManagement/Dashboard.jsx, Pages/Logistics/Dashboard.jsx and Pages/Procurement/Dashboard.jsx actually render, in English because the page is English, with illustrative figures the section labels as such. The frame gained the application\'s own chrome bar with its Operating Unit selector, and the tablist finally implements the pattern it claimed: arrow keys move, Home/End jump, roving tabindex, aria-controls on the panel. ONE LANGUAGE. The page was English apart from two blocks -- the Permit To Work chain and the four plan framing lines -- so a visitor scrolling from the hero changed language halfway down. Both are English now, and the PTW chain gained the thing that makes it an argument rather than a list of nine steps: the DEPARTMENT that owns each step, so the record is visibly crossing Field, People and HSE without anybody rekeying it. A test pins the page against the specific Indonesian strings that were removed. MOTION, PERFORMANCE AND ACCESSIBILITY. Two new keyframes (dataflow, hub-ring), both plain CSS in tailwind.config.js beside the existing ones, both reached only through Tailwind\'s motion-safe: variant -- verified in the built stylesheet: every animation utility on this page sits inside @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference). Verified in the browser with animations forced off: zero elements at opacity 0 or visibility hidden, 7,966 characters of text still readable. No animation library was added; the bundle grew 427.4KB to 431.3KB gzipped for four redesigned sections. No horizontal overflow at 375px or at desktop, no console errors, every request 200. Tests: 249 -> 252 (1123 assertions), including the ladder derivation, the shell-workspace rule and the one-language rule. No migration. No pricing value, entitlement mapping, checkout, payment gateway, webhook verification, tenant isolation, RBAC or provisioning behaviour was changed.'],
        ['version' => '2.60.0', 'date' => '2026-09-26', 'summary' => 'THE FOUR-TIER PRICING MODEL, and the two silent failures the shape of the old one was hiding. IOMS sold three plans and the ladder did not match the product: measured by implemented route prefixes, Starter (HSE, 31) was already most of the platform, Professional added ONE department (HR, 11) for 3.3x the price, and Enterprise added eight more for 2x. The expensive step was the small one, and there was nothing between a single-department plan and the whole platform for the customer who runs projects, materials and procurement across a few sites -- which is most industrial operations. The approved catalogue is now Starter Rp299.000, Professional Rp799.000, Business Rp1.499.000 and Enterprise Rp2.499.000 per month, annual at ten months on every tier (16.67%, shown as 17%), with capacity climbing 10/50/150/unlimited users and 1/2/4/unlimited Operating Units. Professional goes DOWN and Enterprise goes UP: the multipliers between tiers now decrease as the absolute price rises (2.67x, 1.88x, 1.67x) instead of spiking in the middle. BUSINESS IS THE POINT OF THE RELEASE -- the first tier that crosses departments, adding Project Management, Logistics / PPIC and Procurement to HSE and People, and the largest scope jump in the catalogue at the middle price. WHAT A PLAN GRANTS NO LONGER LIVES IN A MATCH STATEMENT. Package::defaultWorkspaceKeys() was a hardcoded match with a default => [] arm, so a plan created through the Platform Admin UI, or one whose slug was mistyped, resolved to ZERO departments while still carrying a price -- the v2.58.0 empty-sidebar defect one layer up, and adding a fourth slug is exactly the change that trips it. config/plans.php now holds tier scope, positioning and the recommended flag in one file, and an unrecognised slug falls back to the ENTRY TIER rather than to nothing: a customer gets a working, clearly bounded product and the operator sees an obviously wrong plan, instead of a signed-up customer staring at an application with no navigation. REPRICING A CATALOGUE MUST NOT REPRICE A CUSTOMER. Subscriptions now carry agreed_price_monthly / agreed_price_yearly / agreed_currency, stamped at provisioning and backfilled from a literal table of pre-v2.60 prices -- literal because migrations run in filename order and the repricing migration runs FIRST, so reading the live packages table would have backfilled every existing customer at the NEW price, which is the exact error the column exists to prevent. Verified against the real database: the existing Enterprise tenant stays at Rp19.990.000 rather than jumping to Rp24.990.000, and the billing page now says so in words -- "Your agreed price, kept from when you subscribed. This plan is now published at Rp24.990.000." -- instead of leaving a customer to notice the discrepancy themselves. THE DEAD FEATURE LIST IS GONE. packages.features was a third, non-authoritative list of what a plan includes, sitting beside the two that are actually enforced (the Module and Workspace grant tables) and drifting from them. v1.11.15 had already established nothing read it; it is dropped from the schema rather than left dormant for somebody to reach for, and hasFeature() with it. FOUR COLUMNS, WITHOUT CROWDING. Every public pricing surface -- landing, /pricing, Get Started, the authenticated Plans page -- shows four tiers: one-up on mobile, two-up from md, four-up at xl, because three across at lg leaves a lone card on a second row and four at lg makes each card narrower than its own price line. Business carries a restrained "Most popular" label, which is finally honest: v2.27.0 refused to print one because no such signal existed in the Package data, and the answer was to put the signal in config where the UI can read it rather than to keep guessing from a card index -- the guess (plans.length === 3 ? 1 : -1) would have silently emphasised nothing the moment a fourth tier arrived. Pricing cards now list DEPARTMENTS rather than the full provisioning grant: Reports and Settings belong to every plan and printing them spent two of six visible bullets saying nothing about the tier. FOUND BY LOOKING AT THE RUNNING PAGE: Professional was still advertising a 14-day free trial, because the repricing migration normalised trial_days on INSERT but not on UPDATE -- and every existing tier takes the update path. IOMS does not sell a trial; a test now pins that for the whole catalogue. ALSO IN THE TESTS: the approved figures had been transcribed into three separate test files, and repricing broke all three at once -- the failure mode those tests exist to prevent, reproduced in the tests themselves. Tests\Support\ApprovedCatalogue is now the one place they are written down. Tests: 225 -> 249 (1083 assertions). Three migrations, none destructive: no plan is deleted, every live subscription keeps its package_id, and the rollback removes Business only if nothing is subscribed to it. No tenant isolation, RBAC, entitlement enforcement, payment gateway, webhook verification or provisioning behaviour was changed -- the webhook remains the sole authority for activating a paid subscription.'],
        ['version' => '2.59.0', 'date' => '2026-09-25', 'summary' => 'Landing page experience and brand consistency. THE PRODUCT SHOWCASE WAS THE WEAKEST THING ON THE SITE, and it was a positioning problem rather than a styling one. The section titled "What working in IOMS looks like" rendered two panels: an HSE dashboard whose four figures were literally the character "--" above a dashed empty rectangle, and a permit with three grey bars where its content should be. A visitor evaluating industrial software saw placeholder furniture and no product at all. Worse, those two panels were the ENTIRE product visualisation and both were HSE/PTW -- a page selling an Industrial Operations Platform showed one department and called it the product. PlatformShowcase replaces it with five real workspaces inside one application frame, switched by tabs: Dashboard, Health Safety & Environment, Warehouse, Procurement, Maintenance. PTW is now one row inside one of five tabs, which is its true proportion. The frame deliberately reuses the authenticated product own visual language -- navy rail, navy page header with an eyebrow, compact stat chips with filled icon chips, uppercase micro-labels -- so a visitor who signs up recognises what they were shown, and every label is real IOMS vocabulary: Operating Unit, Permit To Work, Material Request, Purchase Order, Work Order, NCR, CAPA, Goods Receipt. No capability is implied that IOMS does not have, and the sample figures are stated as illustrative in the section own subtitle. A test pins the module set and the vocabulary so a later edit cannot quietly narrow it back to one department. MOTION, AND THE BUG THAT SHAPED IT. The page had none; it now has one gesture -- a short rise and fade as a block enters the viewport -- applied through a single component so the whole page inherits it and there is one place to change or remove it. No animation library: Framer Motion is roughly 50KB gzipped for what an IntersectionObserver and one keyframe already do, and the entire reveal system cost about 6KB. The first implementation rendered elements at opacity 0 and transitioned them in. Browser testing found the failure: IntersectionObserver reports threshold CROSSINGS, not positions, so an element that moves from below the viewport to above it within a single frame -- a programmatic jump, an in-page anchor, the browser restoring scroll position on reload -- was never intersecting at either sample, no callback fired, and it stayed invisible permanently. One scrollTo() past a heading proved it. Rather than patching that with scroll listeners, which only narrows the window, the state was inverted: the element is ALWAYS VISIBLE and entering the viewport merely ADDS a one-shot keyframe. A failed, skipped or unsupported observer now costs the animation and nothing else. Verified: after an instant jump to the bottom of the page, zero headings remain hidden. Reduced motion is guarded twice -- every class is behind motion-safe:, and the hook creates no observer at all when the visitor prefers reduced motion. BACKGROUND ATMOSPHERE. Every navy band used the same treatment, a 56px grid plus two blurred colour blobs, so three dark sections read as one flat blue field repeated -- which was the specific weakness, not a lack of decoration but a lack of depth. BlueprintBackdrop replaces it with a finer technical grid, ONE light source rather than two competing blobs, and an abstract industrial silhouette drawn as flat geometry: gantry crane with bracing, storage tanks, structural frames, a stack. Inline SVG and CSS only -- no image request, nothing to load, scales to any viewport, and aria-hidden throughout because it is texture. ALSO FOUND BY LINT: removing the placeholder mockups looked safe, but MockupFieldHome was rendered by a DIFFERENT section further up the file. npm run lint caught it immediately -- the second real defect that narrow config has caught since v2.54.0. It was rebuilt properly as a phone-shaped My Work preview showing the real ordering the page copy describes: greeting, action tiles first, running permits below. VERIFIED IN THE BROWSER: five workspace tabs render and switch, no horizontal overflow at 375px or desktop, the workspace switcher scrolls horizontally on mobile as intended, no console errors, every request 200, and no content stranded after an instant scroll jump. Bundle grew 420.9KB to 427.2KB gzipped for the showcase, the reveal system and the backdrop combined. Tests: 222 -> 225 (887 assertions). No migration. The v2.58.0 email design system needed NO changes -- it was reviewed and the centralised logo partial is still the single swap point for the official mark. No tenant isolation, RBAC, entitlement, payment, webhook, provisioning, pricing or authentication behaviour was touched.'],
        ['version' => '2.58.0', 'date' => '2026-09-24', 'summary' => 'THE EMPTY SIDEBAR, and it was two layers disagreeing rather than one bug. A self-service customer could sign in, reach the Dashboard, and see a navy sidebar with nothing in it. EntitlementService::tenantCanUseWorkspace() treats "this tenant has no grant rows" as UNRESTRICTED -- deliberate and documented, so a tenant provisioned before grants existed is never locked out of its own product. HandleInertiaRequests independently plucked the same pivot and forced every workspace not in the list to is_active:false, so an empty list meant NOTHING GRANTED. The route layer let the customer through and the navigation layer showed them no way to get there; neither piece of code was wrong alone, they had simply never been asked to agree. COMPOUNDING IT, AND THE REASON EVERY STARTER AND PROFESSIONAL CUSTOMER WAS AFFECTED: reports and administration carry tier=global -- Settings, Users, Audit Logs, Report Center -- and the sidebar global state, the state you are in ON THE DASHBOARD, is built from exactly those two. Package::defaultWorkspaceKeys() listed departments only, so no plan granted them. Both keys also appear in config(departments), and workspace entitlement enforcement is ON by default, so those same customers were additionally 403d out of Reports, Analytics, Report Center and Audit Logs. Enterprise was the only plan that escaped, because it granted every workspace -- which is exactly why this survived every previous verification pass: the development tenant is on Enterprise. THE FIX IS ARCHITECTURAL, NOT A PATCH. workspaces.tier already encoded the distinction the entitlement layer was ignoring; Workspace::globalKeys()/isGlobalKey() name it, and EntitlementService::grantedWorkspaceKeys() is now the SINGLE answer to "what may this tenant reach", called by both the route gate and the navigation layer so they cannot drift apart again. Its rules, in order: a global-tier workspace is always granted; a tenant with no grant rows at all is unrestricted, matching the documented entitlement default; otherwise the explicit grants plus the global keys. Package::defaultWorkspaceKeys() unions the global keys into every plan, and an unrecognised slug no longer means "no product". DEPARTMENTS ARE STILL SOLD AND STILL RESTRICTED -- a test asserts a Starter tenant still does not receive Warehouse or Human Resources, in the same run that asserts its sidebar is not empty. AUDITED ACROSS CONTEXTS: Starter, Professional, Enterprise, an unrecognised plan slug, a tenant with zero grants, a Super Admin, a Department User, and the real self-service tenant in the local database, which went from two workspaces to four. DASHBOARD DENSITY. Ten oversized cards stood between a customer and their charts. "Current Period" is gone -- it was never a metric, it restated the year/month selector a few pixels above it and cost a quarter of the row to do so. "Companies" became "Operating Units", the vocabulary the product has used since v2.54.0; only the label was stale. Company Snapshot and Needs Attention moved to the compact card scale, and the release-announcement panel -- six changelog lines in a two-column grid, above a customer operational figures, on every release -- is now one expandable line. Roughly a full band of vertical space returned, with no dashboard metric or business meaning changed. THE IOMS EMAIL DESIGN SYSTEM. One shell with three partials (logo, button, summary), so no template draws its own header, call-to-action or key/value table. The shell adds a hidden preheader (without it, clients scrape the first visible words, which were identical in every IOMS message), a 3px tone band coloured by purpose, an eyebrow category, and a footer carrying the reply mailbox plus Terms, Privacy, Refund and Contact. Contextual variation is the tone band and the eyebrow and deliberately nothing more: a transactional email is read in four seconds by somebody who wants one fact, not a newsletter. Table-based and inline-styled throughout, because Outlook renders no flex or grid; the single style block holds only media queries. THE LOGO LIVES IN EXACTLY ONE FILE. partials/logo.blade.php renders a typographic wordmark today and carries the swap instructions for the official mark arriving tomorrow -- absolute https URL, explicit width and height, keep the alt text, keep the wordmark as the fallback for the roughly half of clients that block remote images. Nothing else in the email system needs to change when it lands. SELF-SERVICE PROVISIONING VERIFIED, NOT REDESIGNED: TenantProvisioningService creates the Organization, the first Operating Unit, an active Subscription on the right cycle, the package workspace and module grants, the Super Admin account using the password hashed at registration, the company document identity, and attaches the pre-provisioning invoice -- all from the verified webhook, with no manual operator step. The one defect in that chain was the workspace grant mapping, fixed here. Tests: 210 -> 222 (874 assertions). No migration. Browser-verified as the real self-service Professional customer: the sidebar renders Reports, Analytics, Report Center, Users, Departments, Positions, Operating Units, Settings, Module Management and Audit Logs. No tenant isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, payment architecture, webhook verification, subscription state machine or Organization/Operating Unit behaviour was changed.'],
        ['version' => '2.57.0', 'date' => '2026-09-23', 'summary' => 'Production email configuration, and two defects that only a live mail transport would ever have exposed. AUDITED FIRST, AND THE ARCHITECTURE WAS ALREADY RIGHT. Four mailboxes in one config block, three Mailables all sending From noreply with a Reply-To matching the conversation -- billing for an invoice, support for an activation, sales for a signup verification -- and no credential anywhere in source. None of that was rebuilt. What the audit found was the mail that does NOT go through those Mailables. THE PASSWORD RESET EMAIL WAS STOCK LARAVEL. Laravel\'s built-in ResetPassword notification: scaffolding wording, no IOMS letterhead, no Reply-To at all, and a From address taken from MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS rather than from the IOMS mailbox configuration every other IOMS email uses. It is the single message a SaaS sends most often, and it would have gone live unbranded the instant the SMTP password was entered. It is now an ordinary Mailable on the same emails.layout shell as the other three, routed in by one override of User::sendPasswordResetNotification() -- not a second mail system. Reply-To is support, because somebody who cannot get into their account needs a human rather than an unmonitored sender. THE GLOBAL FROM ADDRESS WAS TWO RENAMES STALE. The local .env still carried hse@shipyard.local, and IOMS had no config/mail.php at all, so the framework default read MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS and nothing connected it to config(ioms.emails). The three explicit Mailables were unaffected; anything WITHOUT an explicit From -- which is exactly what the password reset was -- went out under it. A From address the sending domain does not authorise is the ordinary reason transactional mail lands in spam. config/mail.php is now published for that one reason: the From address CHAINS to IOMS_NOREPLY_EMAIL, so configuring the four mailboxes is sufficient and there is no second place to set the sender. Everything else in that file is the framework default, copied unchanged. MAIL_ENCRYPTION DOES NOT EXIST IN LARAVEL 11+. The shipped mail config has no encryption key, so MAIL_ENCRYPTION=ssl would have been set and silently ignored. Implicit TLS on port 465 comes from the SCHEME: Laravel infers smtps from the port, and MAIL_SCHEME=smtps states it outright so the setting survives a later port change. Documented as such rather than repeating a dead variable, which reads as configured when it is not. BOTH DEFECTS WERE FOUND BY A TEST, NOT BY READING. The new suite asserts the ENVELOPE of every Mailable -- From, Reply-To, subject -- which needs no transport, no credential and no network; asserts the password broker actually routes through the IOMS Mailable rather than Laravel\'s own; asserts no customer-facing identity carries @ioms.id, @ioms.local or shipyard.local while leaving the seeded @ioms.local development accounts alone; and asserts every credential key in .env.example ships empty, so a filled one is a failing test rather than a quiet commit. PRODUCTION CONFIGURATION IS DOCUMENTED AS TWO LISTS, deliberately: the SMTP host, port, scheme and username are configuration and are committed; MAIL_PASSWORD is the single mail secret and is entered on the server only. README carries both, the Reply-To table, and a one-line delivery check to run after the password is in place. Tests: 199 -> 210 (837 assertions). No migration. No secret added: every credential key in the repository is still empty. No production Midtrans credentials exist and none are implied. No payment architecture, webhook verification, tenant isolation, RBAC, subscription state machine or entitlement behaviour was touched.'],
        ['version' => '2.56.0', 'date' => '2026-09-22', 'summary' => 'Checkout corrections after the v2.55.0 review, and one pricing derivation that stopped being duplicated. THE ANNUAL DISCOUNT WAS INVISIBLE WHERE IT MATTERED MOST. The Pricing page said "17% less than monthly"; Get Started and the checkout order summary showed only the final annual figure. So a buyer choosing a cycle, and then a buyer about to pay, both had to work out for themselves that the annual price was already discounted -- at the two moments they were deciding. All three surfaces now state the comparison in full: twelve monthly payments, the annual price, the difference in rupiah, and the percentage. For Professional that reads 12 x Rp999.000 = Rp11.988.000 against Rp9.990.000, saving Rp1.998.000, 17%. THE NUMBER IS DERIVED ONCE, SERVER-SIDE. It used to be a JSX expression inside Pricing.jsx -- correct, but living on one page, so any second surface that wanted it had to copy the arithmetic, and a copied derivation is how two pages eventually disagree about the same price. PricingService::annualSaving() now computes it from the package OWN two prices and every surface reads the result. Nothing is invented: a plan without two real prices, or whose annual price is not actually cheaper, presents no saving at all rather than a zero or a fabricated badge. NO PRICE CHANGED. Starter Rp299.000/Rp2.990.000, Professional Rp999.000/Rp9.990.000, Enterprise Rp1.999.000/Rp19.990.000, with 10/50/highest user accounts and 1/2/multiple Operating Units. A test now pins those figures so an accidental revert to an older discussed price is a failing test rather than a live pricing error, and another walks the whole chain -- landing, Pricing, Get Started, checkout, invoice -- asserting the same amount reaches all five from the packages table. THE ACQUISITION FLOW WAS HALF IN TWO LANGUAGES. Get Started mixed "Akun Anda" and "Paket Anda" with "Full name" and "Work email"; the checkout and the registration status page were entirely Indonesian while the form fields above them were English. All three pages are now consistent English, including the labels the review named -- Your Account, Your Plan, Your Company, Annual Billing, Monthly, Annual, Continue to Payment -- and the surrounding sentences rather than only those. The marketing pages and the legal documents are deliberately unchanged: the policies are substantive Indonesian text for an Indonesian legal audience, and translating them was not part of this correction. ALSO CLEANED: a public password-reset form advertising a local development address as its placeholder, a stale demo-account fallback pointing at the old mail domain, and the forbidden long-form product name still sitting in composer.json description and as the ROADMAP title -- the exact expansion config/ioms.php Canonical Product Identity block exists to keep out of the product. The seeded @ioms.local accounts stay: they are development values and are documented as such. DOMAIN. iomsuite.com is now live in cPanel alongside ioms.web.id, mapped and serving over HTTPS. That is infrastructure and was done manually; what this release adds is the application side of it -- README now carries the exact production .env block (APP_NAME, APP_URL, SANCTUM_STATEFUL_DOMAINS, SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE, TRUSTED_PROXIES, the four mailboxes), the cache commands to run after editing it, the payment notification URL to register against the live domain, and a note that only APP_URL decides which domain generated links use while both domains serve. No secret is included and none belongs in git. Tests: 192 -> 199 (790 assertions). No migration. Browser-verified: Pricing, Get Started with the saving panel, and no horizontal overflow at mobile width. No production Midtrans credentials exist and none are implied. No payment architecture, tenant isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, subscription state machine or entitlement behaviour was changed.'],
        ['version' => '2.55.0', 'date' => '2026-09-21', 'summary' => 'Infrastructure, domain, email and payment-verification readiness -- and deliberately NOT a rebuild of anything that already worked. INSPECTED FIRST, AND MOST OF IT DID. There are ZERO hardcoded ioms.web.id references in application code: APP_URL, SANCTUM_STATEFUL_DOMAINS, SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE and the webhook URL were already environment-driven, and AppServiceProvider already forced https when APP_URL says https. The four-mailbox separation existed and every Mailable already sent From noreply with a Reply-To matching the conversation. The payment architecture -- PaymentGatewayInterface, MidtransGateway on Snap, NullPaymentGateway that throws rather than faking, signature verification before any state is read, PaymentWebhookEvent::recordIfNew() idempotent on (gateway, event_id), the invoice amount re-checked, TenantProvisioningService::activate() idempotent under a row lock and acting only on PAID -- was already correct and is untouched. Get Started was already a real onboarding flow rather than a redirect to Login. So this release changes configuration, fills five genuine gaps, and leaves the architecture alone. DOMAIN. iomsuite.com replaces iomsplatform.com, which was never owned and sat in config as the product website -- an address a customer cannot reach is worse than none. documentation_url now defaults to NULL and the About dialog omits the row entirely, because docs.iomsplatform.com did not exist and a link that 404s costs more trust than an absent one. The migration itself stays an environment change, so ioms.web.id keeps working: nothing in code names either domain. TRUSTED_PROXIES was added because cPanel terminates TLS at a proxy and forwards over plain HTTP, so without it Laravel reads the request as http:// and puts insecure links in password-reset emails; it is opt-in rather than hardcoded to "*", because trusting forwarding headers from an untrusted network lets a client spoof its own scheme and host. EMAIL. The four mailboxes move to @iomsuite.com, MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS in .env.example is aligned with IOMS_NOREPLY_EMAIL (it had been left at a two-renames-ago noreply@ioms.local), and the sender/Reply-To architecture is preserved exactly as it was. THE LEGAL PAGES WERE PLACEHOLDERS READING "This page is being prepared", which is not a document a payment provider can verify a merchant against. Terms of Service, Privacy Policy and a new Refund & Cancellation Policy are now real documents, structured on the concepts a SaaS merchant agreement is expected to cover and written for what IOMS actually is: standardized business-to-business subscription software for industrial operations, sold in IDR, delivered over the web. NOTHING IS FABRICATED, and the mechanism is the point: clauses are written with :token markers in App\Support\LegalDocuments, and a paragraph whose fact is unconfigured is DISCARDED WHOLE rather than printed with a gap that reads like a statement -- so the operator name, business address and governing-law clauses simply do not appear until config(\'ioms.legal\') has them. Sections left empty are dropped too. THE PRIVATE/PUBLIC BOUNDARY IS EXPLICIT. The identity a payment provider verifies -- personal name, residential address, tax number, ID documents -- is merchant KYC data, not website content. IOMS_LEGAL_ADDRESS exists only for an address the operator intends to PUBLISH, and there is deliberately no config key for a tax or registration number at all. A CONTACT PAGE replaces a footer mailto: that opened the operating system "choose an app" dialog and did nothing on a machine with no mail client -- the same acquisition dead end v2.51.0 removed from the pricing CTAs, still sitting in the footer of every page. Three mailboxes with three stated jobs, and no invented postal address or phone number. The footer gains a Legal column, because a subscription buyer looks for terms and refund position BEFORE paying rather than in fine print. THE INVOICE PDF, built on the EXISTING document architecture. An invoice existed only as a row and an email; Indonesian B2B customers file invoices. It reuses pdf.partials.{styles,letterhead,footer} unchanged, with InvoiceDocumentService supplying the ISSUER identity -- because an invoice is the one document in IOMS that runs the other way, and DocumentEngine::identity() would have printed the customer billing themselves. Reachable from both places it needs to be: Settings > Billing for a signed-in administrator, ownership checked on invoices.tenant_id because that table has no company_id and inherits nothing from Company\'s global scopes; and from the registration status page for a prospect who has paid but has no account yet, authorised by the same unguessable token as that page. No tax line: IOMS holds no tax registration in configuration, and an invoice showing an invented NPWP is worse than one that stays silent. CHECKOUT NOW STARTS ON IOMS\'S OWN PAGE. It used to redirect()->away() the instant the customer clicked pay, so their last view before entering payment details was somebody else\'s site. An IOMS order summary now states plan, billing cycle, period, invoice number and the amount in Rupiah, and Snap opens over it using a token the SERVER created. THE ACTIVATION RULE IS UNCHANGED AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT: only the client key reaches the browser and it authorises nothing, every Snap callback does exactly one thing (navigate to the read-only status page), no endpoint accepts a payment result from a browser, and a subscription becomes active only in PaymentWebhookController from a payload the provider signed and this server verified. A test asserts the server key never reaches the page. THE PUBLIC FAQ CONTRADICTED THE PRICING PAGE: it still sold PTW Access as a purchased allowance ("10 Users, 5 PTW Access") a full two releases after that was retired, and never mentioned Operating Units. Corrected, with a new entry explaining the organizational model the capacity story now rests on. Tests: 175 -> 191 (682 assertions), including both directions of invoice ownership and a non-production checkout render. One migration. Browser-verified: the three policy documents, the contact page, the footer, mobile layout at 375px, and a real invoice PDF rendered end to end. No production Midtrans credentials exist and none are implied -- production payment was NOT tested and is not claimed. No tenant isolation, RBAC, authentication, PTW authorization, subscription state machine, entitlement or document-numbering behaviour was changed.'],
        ['version' => '2.54.0', 'date' => '2026-09-20', 'summary' => 'The organizational model, stated once: IOMS -> Organization -> Operating Unit(s) -> Departments. INSPECTED BEFORE BUILDING, and the production data settled it: GAJ and MTC are two `companies` rows under ONE tenant. They were never two customers and were never billed separately -- they are two OPERATING UNITS of a single organization, which is also what ADR-008 meant when it called Company "an internal business unit WITHIN one Tenant". So Tenant IS the Organization and Company IS the Operating Unit; no data moved, nothing was deleted, no table was renamed. Renaming Company would touch every FK, scope and controller in the schema to buy a word. What changed is that the levels now have their names and are used consistently on every human-readable surface: 92 labels across 51 pages, the sidebar, the Settings tab, the pricing cards and the plan descriptions. The exclusions were the whole job -- a contractor firm, a visitor employer, the second party on a BAST and the legal company typed at signup are NOT operating units, and each was read in context and left alone. LEGAL ENTITY IS AN ATTRIBUTE, NOT A LEVEL. Most customers trade as one registered company and run several sites; a few Enterprise customers have several entities. Modelling that as a fourth table would impose a join on everyone to serve the minority, and would create a second competing owner of "who does this record belong to" beside company_id. Two nullable columns say the same thing and change no query: an operating unit MAY declare the entity it trades as, and documents may print it. Nothing is scoped by it. CAPACITY IS MEASURED IN OPERATING UNITS, AND IS NOW ENFORCED. packages.max_companies was read for DISPLAY only -- the Settings meter drew from it and the pricing page printed it -- while storeCompanyEntity() created units with no check at all: the same revenue leak v2.38.0 found on user seats, in the other quota. Enforced now inside a row-locked transaction, the same shape already proven on user creation. Professional moves from one operating unit to two, a recorded commercial decision: it is the tier for an organization opening a second yard, which is the commonest reason to outgrow Starter. It never meant two companies you may bill separately, and the pricing page now says so in words. The usage count deliberately bypasses global scopes -- a quota is a property of the organization, never of the person asking, and counting through the scoped model would let a restricted administrator count 1 and create past the limit. PER-OPERATING-UNIT AUTHORIZATION BECAME REACHABLE. CompanyAuthorizationScope and the company_user pivot shipped correct in v2.53.0, but NOTHING COULD WRITE TO THAT PIVOT: the authorization existed and every user was unrestricted in practice. Settings > Users now grants it, server-validated against the current organization, with an all-units selection stored as "no restriction" rather than a full list so a unit added later does not silently exclude everyone. An empty submission still means every unit, never none. Browser testing then found a real trap and it was fixed: an administrator who restricted themselves lost the very column and tab that would have undone it, because those were drawn from the scoped list. Organization administration now reads the unscoped roster; operational selectors stay scoped. A SECOND PRODUCTION-BREAKING DEFECT, FOUND WHILE WRITING THE TESTS FOR THE FIRST. companies.name and companies.code have carried GLOBAL unique indexes since the table was created in a single-company world. Multi-tenancy arrived in Milestone 2 and nobody revisited them -- even though storeCompanyEntity() validates per tenant and its own comment asserts two tenants CAN both have a company named GAJ. They could not. This is the v2.53.0 document-number defect again, and it landed somewhere worse: TenantProvisioningService::activate() runs from a VERIFIED PAYMENT WEBHOOK and creates the first operating unit named after the registration, with a code taken from the first six characters of the slugged name. Two customers called "PT Maju Jaya" collide on the name index; "PT Bahari Nusantara" and "PT Bahari Sejahtera" both give PTBAHA and collide on the code index; a name that slugs to nothing hits the literal COMP fallback. In every case the insert throws INSIDE PROVISIONING, AFTER THE PAYMENT IS CONFIRMED -- invoice issued, no tenant, no login, and a duplicate key is not transient so nothing retries. Both indexes are now composite on (tenant_id, ...). EVERY CHECKBOX IN IOMS WAS INVISIBLE. ui/checkbox.jsx sized its root with h-4.5 w-4.5, and Tailwind default spacing scale carries half-steps only up to 3.5 -- so those classes generated NO CSS AT ALL and the control collapsed to zero size. Forty-nine checkboxes across twenty files: module visibility, role permissions, PPE selection. Still there, still clickable, with nothing to see and no way to tell checked from unchecked. Fixed by defining the 18px step rather than rewriting the classes, so the three call sites that meant 18px get it and a fourth cannot fail the same way. A WHOLE SETTINGS TAB WAS DEAD. Settings/Index.jsx read canFieldAccess inside a module-scope function where it was never a prop, so opening Settings > Users threw a ReferenceError and blanked the page -- invisible to every test because Radix only mounts the active tab content. Found by lint, which leads to: npm run lint HAD BEEN IN package.json FOR A YEAR AND NEVER RAN, because there was no eslint.config.js and ESLint 9 needs flat config. There is one now, deliberately narrow -- undefined identifiers, unreachable code, duplicate keys, JSX referencing an undefined component -- because a lint run that reports thousands of style findings is one nobody reads. FORM AUDIT ACROSS EVERY MODULE: every data-entry form paired its fields with a bare grid-cols-2 or grid-cols-3, which has no breakpoint prefix and therefore applies at 320px exactly as at 1920px. A permit start and end datetime, an employee NIK and name, a requisition three dates -- all squeezed into ~150px columns on the phone a field worker actually files them from. Fifty sites across nineteen forms now start at one column and pair from sm: up; the three-way Pass/Fail/N-A toggle is the deliberate exception. Eleven forms that pair fields were widened from max-w-xl, which capped each half of a pair narrower than the single column it replaced. Tests: 158 -> 175 (592 assertions). Two migrations, both run against MySQL 8.4. Browser-verified: the Operating Units tab and its capacity meter, per-unit grants written and revoked with the audit trail, the restored Settings > Users tab, visible checkboxes, the organizational context in the header, pricing reading 1/2/Multiple Operating Units, and forms stacking correctly at 375px. No tenant isolation, RBAC, payment verification, subscription state machine, PTW authorization or Master Admin boundary was weakened.'],
        ['version' => '2.53.0', 'date' => '2026-09-19', 'summary' => 'Product, UX, content and SaaS consistency pass -- and a production-breaking multi-tenant defect found while doing it. THE DEFECT: fourteen document-number columns (ptw_number, po_number, request_number, task_number and the rest) carried a GLOBAL unique index while their counters became PER-TENANT in v2.41.0. Those two facts cannot both hold -- every tenant\'s counter starts at 1, so every tenant\'s first permit is PTW-YYYY-00001 and only the first tenant in the database could ever have it. The SECOND customer to sign up could not create their first Permit To Work, Material Request, Purchase Order or Task at all; the insert failed on a duplicate key. v2.41.0\'s own note claimed the opposite and went unchallenged because every environment had one tenant with real data. It surfaced the moment a second tenant seeded a permit. Each index is now composite on (company_id, number) -- a document number is unique WITHIN a company, exactly like an invoice number. goods_receipts is left alone and recorded, because it has no company_id to scope by. MY WORK: actions first, information second. v2.52.0 put live permits above the action tiles, which inverts what a field worker opens the page for -- someone on a dock with a phone wants the thing they came to do within thumb\'s reach, and consults the running permits second. The greeting now addresses the crew ("Good Morning, Team.") instead of reading "Good Evening, HSE", which is a mailbox greeting and wrong for a foreman. LANGUAGE HIERARCHY, corrected in both directions. v2.52.0 over-translated: module names, table headers, status labels and buttons became Indonesian, which is the layer that must stay English so a user can say "open Regulations & Standards" out loud and have it match the screen. The rule now applied throughout -- ENGLISH for module and feature names, navigation, column headers, action labels and operational terminology; INDONESIAN for explanatory sentences, help text and guidance. The public marketing site is predominantly English. PTW ACCESS IS NO LONGER A SOLD CAPACITY. It was a second seat pool alongside max_users, so every plan card read as two numbers a buyer had to reconcile ("10 users and 5 PTW Access -- is that fifteen?"). Capacity is now TOTAL USERS, one number. Only the QUOTA is retired: users.ptw_access, User::canCreatePtw() and PermitToWorkController\'s server-side gate are untouched, so nobody gained the ability to raise a permit. Professional is a ONE-company plan; it had advertised five, which is neither the commercial decision nor what the tier is for. ENTERPRISE MULTI-COMPANY is now real authorization rather than an absence of one: a company_user pivot plus CompanyAuthorizationScope layered on TenantScope, so a yard manager at GAJ no longer reaches MTC\'s purchase orders. The default is "no grants = all companies in the tenant", so upgrading changes nothing and no customer is locked out by a migration; authorization becomes real the moment an administrator grants a user their first company, and it only ever removes access. An earlier version of that scope skipped in console context, which also disabled it under PHPUnit -- the feature would have shipped unverified; it now guards on the absence of an authenticated user, which is both narrower and the real condition. PTW FORM: four concepts, four fields, named for what they hold. Project (optional Project Master), Work / Job Reference (the operational identity of THIS work), Work Location / Area (where), Work Description (what). They had overlapped badly -- a "Project / Job" selector directly above a "Nama Pekerjaan / Job" text field, so users wrote compound answers covering both. project_name is renamed work_reference for the same reason: the old name read as "the name of the project". The form went from a narrow single-column ribbon to paired fields on a wider column, and Work Description moved after the dates, where it belongs once what and where are settled. Starter still raises permits with no Project Management and no Project Master. EQUIPMENT MASTER vs REGISTER: both tables existed, unconnected. safety_equipment.type was free text beside an hse_equipment_types master that nothing referenced, so "Gas Detector" and "Gas detector" were different types; the register had no identifier of its own (a yard runs GD-001, GD-002, not "a gas detector"); and one lifecycle date served every kind of equipment. Which lifecycles apply is a property of the TYPE -- a gas detector is calibrated, a fire extinguisher expires, a blower is serviced -- so the master declares them and the register carries only the dates that apply. Two tabs now, not one scrolling list. IOMS SANDBOX, deliberately NOT a free trial. A free trial hands someone their own empty tenant and runs the whole provisioning, entitlement and billing machinery for people who may never buy -- and an empty workspace is a poor demonstration. The Sandbox is one shared, pre-populated tenant flagged is_demo, seeded with an operation that looks like it has been running: departments, people, projects, permits in flight, equipment on its inspection cycle. It is a REAL tenant, so TenantScope, RBAC and PTW authorization apply to it unchanged; RestrictDemoTenant adds read-mostly on top via an allow-list, so a new destructive route added later is refused by default rather than silently reachable. Two departments are genuinely interactive, the rest is browsable, and Settings/billing/Master Admin are refused outright with a message that routes to Pricing. LANDING PAGE repositioned from "an HSE product with modules attached" to a connected Industrial Operations Platform: the problem is stated as departments not sharing data they already hold, the domains lead with Project Management and Operations rather than HSE, and Permit To Work is framed as one worked example of the chain rather than the product\'s defining feature. GLOBAL HEADER carries the navy identity the rail and Dashboard already had, with every control inside it flipped to light-on-dark -- the shell now belongs to IOMS rather than to whichever page is open. Tests: 147 -> 158 (557 assertions). Six migrations, all verified against MySQL 8.4. No tenant isolation, RBAC, payment verification, subscription state machine, PTW authorization or Master Admin boundary was weakened.'],
        ['version' => '2.52.0', 'date' => '2026-09-18', 'summary' => 'Consolidated product revision -- domain semantics, field workspace, capacity model, regulatory register, and Bahasa Indonesia public site. BAST vs GOODS RECEIPT: v2.51.0 titled the Goods Receipt PDF "Berita Acara Serah Terima Barang", collapsing two different business documents into one. A Goods Receipt is a WAREHOUSE TRANSACTION whose consequence is a stock level; a BAST is a FORMAL HANDOVER INSTRUMENT between two named parties whose consequence is contractual. A completed Work Order handed to the asset owner needs a BAST and moves no stock at all. HandoverRecord now exists as its own record with its own BAST numbering series, its own narrative document (parties, statement, signatures), and a polymorphic source pointing at the Work Order, Purchase Order, Goods Receipt or Project that already holds the business data -- reuse, not duplication, with the morph target restricted to a closed allow-list so a crafted request cannot aim it at any model. An accepted BAST cannot be deleted: it is evidence. The receiving document is retitled Bukti Penerimaan Barang and no longer asserts contractual acceptance. MY WORK IS A WORKSPACE, PTW ACCESS IS A PERMISSION, and conflating them was the second semantic error. Field routing had been inferred from department_key, which the code itself called a documented MVP proxy that would catch office staff and miss multi-department foremen. users.is_field_user now says the thing directly and decides which WORKSPACE an account lands in; ptw_access continues to decide whether it may CREATE a permit. They are independent in both directions and tested as such -- a field worker normally has the first and not the second, a foreman may have both, and an HSE officer may hold PTW authority without being a field account. Granting the field workspace consumes no PTW seat and grants no capability. My Work itself stopped being a wall of shortcut tiles and became an execution surface: live permits with their job name and work location, assigned tasks with overdue state, and an honest explanation when PTW Access is absent rather than one silently missing button. CAPACITY MODEL stated once and enforced: max_users is how many LOGIN ACCOUNTS a tenant may create and max_ptw_users is how many OF THOSE may be granted PTW Access -- a subset, never an additional pool, so "10 Users / 5 PTW Access" is ten accounts and not fifteen. The migration asserts max_ptw_users <= max_users, clamps any custom plan that violates it, and fills a NULL PTW allowance on a capped plan, because null means UNLIMITED to the entitlement layer. The pricing page now says this in words on every card. PROJECT IDENTITY AND WORK LOCATION are different facts and a permit needs both. permits_to_work.project_name carries the work identity when no formal Project Master exists, so HSE is never blocked on Management creating a project row and a permit for real work never prints "No Project". A registered project still takes precedence when one is selected. REGULATIONS & STANDARDS REGISTER for Health, Safety & Environment: category and document type are free text with suggestions rather than enums, because a provincial regulation, a pressure-vessel rule, an ISO standard and an internal standard all have to fit; superseded_by records what replaced an entry; review_date makes it a living register rather than a list typed once. Attachments go to the PRIVATE disk through the existing SecureDocumentController, which re-checks tenant ownership on every download. A reference seeder offers well-known Indonesian instruments as a starting point, every entry created due-for-review so a human confirms applicability -- deliberately NOT wired into DatabaseSeeder, because a compliance register nobody reviewed that LOOKS reviewed is worse than an empty one. IOMS EMAIL: four mailboxes (support@, billing@, noreply@, hello@) centrally configured and environment-overridable, with each surface routed to the right one -- sales for the marketing pages, billing for invoices, support for a new customer, noreply as the From on automated mail. PUBLIC SITE is Bahasa Indonesia for every explanatory paragraph while product names, department names and established terms (Permit To Work, HIRADC, JSA, LOTO, PPIC) stay English, because translating those reads as less professional to this buyer, not more. The approved visual direction is untouched. Two more mailto acquisition dead ends were found and removed: the landing hero primary CTA pointed at the LOGIN form, and each landing pricing card fell back to a mailto "Talk to Us" whenever a support address existed. DEPARTMENT NAMES are the full English name -- Health, Safety & Environment, Human Resources -- in the navigation registry, the workspace table and the marketing copy. Keys are untouched, because every grant row, route prefix and entitlement check is keyed on them. LAUNCH PRICING: Starter Rp299.000/Rp2.990.000 with 10 users and 5 PTW Access, Professional Rp999.000/Rp9.990.000 with 50 and 20, Enterprise Rp1.999.000/Rp19.990.000 at the highest standardized capacity. EXCEL: the shared foundation now also covers the employee import template, the file customers fill in and send back. Tests: 129 -> 146 (525 assertions). Five migrations, all verified against MySQL 8.4. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, payment, entitlement or workflow logic weakened.'],
        ['version' => '2.51.0', 'date' => '2026-09-17', 'summary' => 'SaaS Product Finalization -- onboarding, payments, and the document identity system. THE PRICING DEFECT, TRACED TO ITS ACTUAL SOURCE: v2.50.0 changed PackageSeeder and every pricing surface, and production still rendered "Gratis / Rp490 / Rp1.490" for a whole release. A seeder is not the runtime source of truth -- the packages TABLE is, and db:seed had never re-run. Everything looked correct in review because it was correct; only the row was stale. Fixed in a migration, which every environment runs once on deploy, and a test now re-runs that migration against deliberately-stale placeholder rows so the gap cannot reopen. Verifying it in a browser then exposed worse drift in the same rows: max_ptw_users was NULL on every plan, and null means UNLIMITED to the entitlement layer, so every tier had been silently granting unlimited PTW Access seats -- an entitlement defect hiding behind a cosmetic one. Starter max_users was 10 against a 15-seat PTW allowance, an impossible combination since PTW seats are a subset of user accounts. The migration now standardizes the whole plan definition. LAUNCH PRICING: Starter Rp499.000/Rp4.990.000, Professional Rp999.000/Rp9.990.000, Enterprise Rp1.999.000/Rp19.990.000. Annual is presented as better value using the saving computed from each plan OWN two prices (17%), never an invented badge. SELF-SERVICE ONBOARDING replaces the mailto handoff, which opened the operating system "choose an app" dialog and did nothing at all on a machine with no mail client -- asking a prospect to compose an email in order to buy software is not an acquisition flow. A prospect now registers through a real form, confirms their email, reviews the plan and pays. TenantRegistration holds the prospect OUTSIDE the tenant isolation boundary: no Tenant, no Company and no User row exists until payment is confirmed server-side, so "a pending tenant must not have application access" is true by construction rather than by a middleware check that could be bypassed. The password is hashed at registration and the plaintext never reaches the database, so activation needs no credential email. TenantProvisioningService::activate() is the one activation path, idempotent under a row lock, and refuses anything not in the PAID state. MIDTRANS ADAPTER implements the EXISTING PaymentGatewayInterface -- Snap, so no card data ever touches IOMS. It verifies the SHA512 signature over order_id + status_code + gross_amount + serverKey BEFORE reading a single field. PaymentWebhookController is the only route that may mark a payment settled: it records the event first so the unique (gateway, event_id) index decides whether a delivery is the first, re-checks the notified amount against the invoice, and refuses to treat a fraud-challenged capture as paid. A browser reaching a success URL activates nothing -- the status page is read-only by construction. Recurring charging is never assumed: PAYMENT_RECURRING_ENABLED=false means invoice-per-cycle renewal, fully implemented, and the tenant subscription model is identical in both modes. DOCUMENT IDENTITY SYSTEM: each PDF used to reach into CompanySetting for whichever fields its own header wanted, so HSE grew a real letterhead and nothing else had one. DocumentEngine::identity() plus four shared partials (styles, letterhead, signatures, footer) now give every document the generating tenant own letterhead -- name, legal name, logo, address, NPWP, NIB -- and four new documents ride on it: Purchase Order/Surat Pesanan, Purchase Requisition/FPB, Goods Receipt/BAST and Work Order/SPK, all reusing the existing tenant-safe numbering. A signature box renders EMPTY when no name is known rather than inventing an approver. EXCEL gained a shared foundation (navy header, frozen pane, autofilter, A4 print setup) applied without touching how any export produces its data. PUBLIC SITE: Platform, Solutions, How It Works and FAQ were header anchors that silently did nothing from any page but the landing page; they are real pages now, and the mobile nav Pricing link was genuinely broken. HEADERS: 44 pages that hand-rolled their heading now use the shared PageHeader, which was itself brought into the Dashboard blue/navy hierarchy -- stronger steel wash, a real navy-to-brand identity rule visible at every breakpoint, and an optional icon chip using the established filled-chip treatment. TENANT BILLING at /subscription/billing shows plan, cycle, status, renewal, live capacity against real entitlements and full invoice history. MASTER ADMIN gained the onboarding pipeline, flagging the case that matters most -- a customer who has paid but has no workspace -- with a re-provision action that cannot activate an unpaid registration. ALSO FIXED, both found by actually running things rather than reading them: a shipped migration aborted php artisan migrate outright on MySQL 8.4 (information_schema returns ENGINE upper-case, and an auto-generated index name was 69 characters against a 64-character limit), so IOMS could not be installed on 8.4 at all; and the public /platform marketing route would have shadowed the Master Admin dashboard URL. Tests: 102 -> 129 (442 assertions). Three migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement or workflow logic weakened.'],
        ['version' => '2.50.0', 'date' => '2026-09-16', 'summary' => 'SaaS Finalization -- the public front door. THE DEFECT: every public "Get Started" CTA pointed at route(login), which is the one door a prospect without an IOMS account cannot walk through, and pricing existed only as an anchor inside the landing page so there was no URL to send anyone to. Sign-in and acquisition are different journeys and both need a visible door. Added /pricing and /get-started as real public routes sharing the exact PricingService payload the landing section and the authenticated Plans page already use -- one price source, three surfaces, so a price can never disagree with itself. Repointed every acquisition CTA in the shared public chrome (nav, mobile nav, footer, landing final CTA, plan CTA), and made the nav Pricing link a route rather than an anchor that silently did nothing from any non-landing page. The login page now signposts prospects to Get Started / View Plans. PRICING standardized to the recorded commercial model: Starter Rp1.499.000/Rp14.990.000, Professional Rp3.499.000/Rp34.990.000, Enterprise Rp7.499.000/Rp74.990.000. The seeder previously refused to touch prices because its own docblock forbade changing them without a documented business decision -- that decision now exists and is recorded in the file, so the guard was satisfied rather than ignored; the old 0/49/149 figures were USD-scale placeholders carrying an IDR label and would have rendered as "Rp49". Enterprise stopped being is_custom: it rendered as "Hubungi Kami" with no price, which contradicts Enterprise being the most complete STANDARDIZED tier rather than a negotiated custom build. IDR now formats as "Rp" rather than the ISO code. Annual is presented as better value using the saving computed from each plan OWN two prices (17%), never an invented badge, and a test asserts annual is genuinely below 12x monthly so the claim cannot silently become false. Plan capability lists are built from the plan real entitlement values (max_users, max_companies, granted workspaces) so the page can never advertise capacity the entitlement layer would not grant. ARCHITECTURE INSPECTED BEFORE BUILDING, and deliberately NOT rebuilt: PaymentGatewayInterface, a swappable PaymentServiceProvider binding, config/payment.php, PaymentTransaction, Invoice, Subscription with a real status model, and PaymentWebhookEvent::recordIfNew() which is already idempotent on (gateway, event_id) all already existed and are correct. NullPaymentGateway throws on every method rather than returning a fake success -- the right default. So no payment code was written: self-serve provisioning and the Midtrans adapter are documented in ROADMAP with the exact external configuration they require (merchant account, server/client keys, a reachable notification URL, and separately-activated Recurring), and /get-started says plainly that a subscription activates only once payment is confirmed. Nothing pretends to provision an account or take a payment. Tests: 95 -> 102 (323 assertions), including a Master Admin boundary assertion in BOTH directions -- a platform admin is redirected out of the tenant app, and a tenant administrator is forbidden from the platform surface. No migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement or workflow changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.49.0', 'date' => '2026-09-15', 'summary' => 'My Work brought into the shared card system, and the root cause of its drift removed. WHY IT WAS LEFT BEHIND: My Work (Field/Home) hand-rolled its tiles from the raw Card primitive plus an inline bg-brand-50/text-brand-600 chip. It never adopted ModuleCard, so none of the v2.43-v2.48 chip and tint work could reach it -- which is exactly why it still showed flat white cards with pale blue icons after every other surface had moved on. The fork itself had a legitimate reason, documented in the file: ModuleCard is a dense 32px enterprise tile, and a field user primary action needs a genuinely easy 48px touch target on a phone, not information density. So rather than either forcing the dense tile onto My Work (a real usability regression for the audience the page exists for) or restyling the fork in place (which would drift again at the next visual pass), ModuleCard gained a `size="lg"` row variant that preserves the 48px target, plus an `accent` prop that tints the surface with the icon own colour. My Work now uses the shared primitive, so future global visual changes reach it automatically. Its information architecture, tiles, order, labels, links and gating are untouched -- presentation only. ModuleCard also learned to accept an already-resolved URL as well as a Ziggy route name, because My Work receives fully-built URLs from the server. Tile accents are derived from the icon the server already sends, keyed in the page, so no controller, route or permission change was needed: incident red, checklist green, observation purple, isolation amber, permits and tasks IOMS blue -- semantics rather than decoration. SWEEP FOR THE SAME PATTERN: two Dashboard sub-components (SummaryStat in Today Alerts, and LeaderboardCard) also hand-rolled their own markup and kept the pale chip. SummaryStat was worse than cosmetic -- its chip was flat graphite regardless of accent, so an alert that mattered looked identical to a calm one; its chip now follows the alert own red/amber/blue accent. LeaderboardCard got the filled chip and the soft steel surface every other entry-point tile carries. No pale-chip signature remains anywhere in Pages or shared components. No iconography, business logic, permission, routing or workflow changes. Verified in the browser: My Work renders 7 tiles with solid 48px filled chips and rgb(255,255,255) glyphs across navy/green/purple accents on matching soft-tinted surfaces, no real horizontal scroll at 320/375/1440, Dashboard unchanged in layout. Tests: 95 passing (258 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.48.0', 'date' => '2026-09-14', 'summary' => 'Focused visual correction: the icon chip and the card surface are drawn from TWO different references, and v2.45.0 had wrongly softened both together. Compared the current implementation against 51c56d7 before changing anything. THE DIAGNOSIS: 51c56d7 chip = a solid accent-filled container with a WHITE glyph and soft depth; current chip = bg-success/15 with a coloured glyph, i.e. a pale tint. 51c56d7 wash = 6-8% (faint); current wash = 13-16% (the visible tint that was preferred). v2.45.0 pulled BOTH down when only the chip coloured GLOW was the actual problem, which produced tint-on-tint -- a pale chip sitting on a lightly tinted card, with nothing in the composition carrying contrast. THE CORRECTION: chip restored from 51c56d7 (solid gradient fill, white glyph, subtle depth) while the surface keeps its current visible wash. The chip now supplies the dark anchor and the surface supplies the soft colour around it, which is the intended relationship. Chip shadows return at ~0.38 alpha rather than 51c56d7 own 0.5-0.55 -- present as depth, short of the glow that was rightly rejected. Applied through the shared components so the relationship is consistent everywhere the pattern appears, not Dashboard-only: StatCard (21 pages, all six accents), ModuleCard (its chip had been flattened to pale steel in the same pass, and its surface now carries the soft steel wash too), KpiSummaryCard (its chip was a 10% tint of the category colour behind a glyph of the SAME colour -- the identical pale problem; now a solid fill of that category own admin-configured colour with a white glyph, and the surrounding surface picks up a soft wash of the same colour so each KPI card is tinted by its own category), and EmptyState (its chip was white-on-white). The two least visible washes (neutral and steel) were brought level with the semantic ones so an unaccented card is genuinely tinted rather than near-white. No layout, iconography, icon set, chart or decoration changes. Verified in the browser on Dashboard, HSE Overview and PTW: chips compute as solid fills with rgb(255,255,255) glyphs over the accent gradient, surfaces keep their soft accent tint, and there is no real horizontal scroll at 320/375/1440. Tests: 95 passing (258 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.47.0', 'date' => '2026-09-13', 'summary' => 'Completion pass for the global visual refresh. TINT AND ICONS: the accent wash moved from ~8-9% to ~14% at the corner easing through ~5%, so it is now clearly present as a coloured surface while staying a wash rather than a fill -- raised by COVERAGE, not saturation, and the hue is still always the accent own (green icon in a green card, amber in amber, purple in purple, red in red, steel when unaccented). Icon chips got a stronger tint behind a DARKER glyph: warning #F59E0B and success #16A34A are mid-tones that go muddy on a tinted chip, so glyphs drop to the 700 step -- defined, not washed out. The chip STYLE (soft tint, coloured glyph, hairline ring) is unchanged, as preferred. WHAT WAS STILL OLD, found by looking at rendered composition rather than at which components a page imports: every list page rendered its search + selects as a bare flex row floating directly on the page background, with a real surface above it (PageHeader) and a real surface below it (the results Card) but nothing holding the filters -- so a list page read as three unrelated fragments instead of one composed screen. That single pattern was the largest remaining marker of the older language. Added a shared FilterBar panel (layout only -- it owns no filter state, no query-string handling and no routing, so every page keeps its existing applyFilters logic) and promoted the filter row on ten list pages: PTW, Daily Reports, Employees, Incidents, KPI Records, Leave, Purchase Requisitions, Reports, Safety Observations and Work Orders. PTW COMPLETE REFRESH, not just its header: filters into the shared panel, permit numbers rendered as monospace navy identifiers so the number anchors each row, table select triggers aligned to the steel border family, the mobile card list given a real hover/press surface instead of a flat divided list, and pagination controls changed from bare bordered squares into proper raised controls. PTW status presentation fixed as a real semantic rather than a colour choice: `submitted` and `pending_approval` mean WAITING ON SOMEONE, and were rendering as a neutral outline chip -- a permit awaiting HSE approval looked the same as an informational label. Both are amber now, matching the same state on the issued PTW document so screen and paper agree. Settings -- the home of PTW Access, and one of the highest-traffic surfaces -- also still hand-rolled its heading on the bare background and now uses the shared panel. VERIFIED IN THE BROWSER: PTW list, detail and create form; PTW Access via Settings > Users; Login; landing page. Login button, PTW New Permit and the landing hero CTA all resolve to the harmonized rgb(33,109,196); all landing dark bands are the single navy rgb(15,39,71); no real horizontal scroll on PTW at 320/375/1440 or on Settings, Login and the landing page. Tests: 95 passing (258 assertions). No migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement, workflow or data-relationship changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.46.0', 'date' => '2026-09-12', 'summary' => 'PTW Access authorization fix + tinted-surface refinement. THE 403, traced route -> middleware -> role -> controller: the HSE sidebar offers "PTW Access" pointing at settings.index?tab=users. That page AND the write behind it (settings.users.ptw-access) are BOTH explicitly gated role:super_admin,hse in routes/web.php, and updatePtwAccess() additionally asserts canManageHse() -- HSE is authorized at every real layer. But config/departments.php mapped the `settings` prefix to `administration` ONLY, and RestrictDepartmentAccess took the FIRST matching department, so a department-scoped HSE user was denied at the ROUTING layer before any of those gates could run. The routing layer was contradicting the authorization layer, the same class of divergence as the v2.42.0 PTW fix. A prefix may now be listed under several departments and `settings` is listed for hse as well as administration. This grants NO new capability and the middleware still fails closed: every mutating settings sub-route (company/branding, modules, roles, companies, backup) stays in the separate role:super_admin group, and a user with department_key=hse but a non-HSE ROLE is still stopped by the route own role gate. Four of the seven new tests exist specifically to prove that -- an unrelated department still cannot open Settings, an HSE user still cannot POST settings.company, an HSE-department user holding a manager role is still blocked, and unrelated department boundaries still hold. VISUAL REFINEMENT, not a revert: the light restrained baseline is kept and only the inside of surfaces changed. v2.45.0 had pulled the StatCard washes down to 4-5%, which on a white card is below the threshold where the eye reads a tint at all, so the cards had gone back to looking flat. The wash now sits around 8-9% at the corner and fades through a mid stop, so it reads as a soft colour wash INSIDE the card rather than a corner blob or a solid coloured card -- and the hue is always the accent own, so a green icon sits in a green card, amber in amber, purple in purple, red in red. The icon treatment from the preferred baseline (soft tint chip, coloured glyph, hairline ring) is deliberately UNCHANGED. Depth stays neutral and restrained: the unused brand-coloured `glow` token was deleted outright, and the two remaining shadow-lift usages dropped to the ordinary card shadow. PAGES BROUGHT FORWARD: PTW Index still hand-rolled a 2xl/bold heading on the bare page background -- the older language, visibly behind every other module page -- and now uses the shared PageHeader panel, so its "New Permit" action sits where every other primary action does. Across the product, 43 pages that hand-rolled the page-title heading were recoloured from graphite to navy ink, matching PageHeader own spec, so page titles are consistent even where the panel is not yet adopted. Verified: PTW "New Permit" and the landing hero CTA both resolve to the harmonized rgb(33,109,196); all landing dark bands are the single navy rgb(15,39,71); PTW title renders navy inside the panel; no real horizontal scroll on Dashboard, PTW, HSE, Settings or the landing page at 320/375/1440. Tests: 88 -> 95 (258 assertions). No migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, entitlement, workflow or data-relationship changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.45.0', 'date' => '2026-09-11', 'summary' => 'Global visual alignment -- soft, layered and atmospheric rather than colourful. This release deliberately PULLS BACK part of v2.44.0. That pass gave every StatCard chip a saturated gradient with its own coloured drop-glow; multiplied across a six-card row it reads as a strip of glowing lozenges, which is loud rather than premium. Chips are now a soft tint carrying a coloured glyph and a hairline ring, surface washes are weaker still, the coloured glows are gone, and the hover lift dropped from the strongest shadow token to the card token. Depth now comes from the tonal step against the ground and the shared shadow, not from saturation -- colour is left doing one job, marking semantics. Same correction applied to ModuleCard chips, the PageHeader accent rule and the Card navy tone. THE REAL SOURCE OF THE BRIGHT BLUE: v2.44.0 re-cut the `brand` SCALE toward the navy/steel axis but left the `--primary` CSS variable behind at the old 221 83% 53%, so every button, focus ring and primary control in the product still resolved to the original bright web blue while the surfaces around them had moved. That is why controls started reading louder than everything else. `--primary` and `--ring` now match brand-600 (212 71% 45%). Buttons were reworked so a primary action is the most SOLID thing on the page rather than the brightest: the weight comes from a navy-tinted shadow and a hairline inner top highlight, not from saturation. Deliberately NOT navy -- a navy button on a navy-anchored shell loses its affordance, and action hierarchy matters more than tonal uniformity. Outline/ghost/secondary moved into the same cool steel family so a secondary beside a primary reads as a quieter sibling rather than a different design system. LANDING PAGE, restructured not recoloured: the hero moved onto a deep navy atmospheric surface sharing the sign-in gateway DNA, and the eight-domain orbit was KEPT and re-lit rather than replaced -- it is the single clearest statement that IOMS is multi-domain and not an HSE tool. Three sections (HSE Workspace / People & Workforce / Data & Insight) were removed because they restated ground the Platform section already covers verbatim -- "HSE Management", "People", "Operations" and "Data & Insight" are literally its own categories -- so the page had five consecutive "here is another module" blocks that both repeated themselves and skewed the positioning toward HSE. 14 sections to 11, 9,362px to ~7,979px, with the two genuine differentiators (the PTW flagship workflow and the field experience) kept. The two dark anchor sections used graphite-900 (#0F172A) while the hero uses navy-900 (#0F2747); three dark bands in one scroll that are almost-but-not-quite the same colour reads as a mistake rather than a system, so all three are navy now with a soft steel or brand wash. Verified: login Sign in and PTW New Permit both resolve to the harmonized rgb(33,109,196); Dashboard stat chips render as 10% tints rather than gradients; the 8 real KPI sparklines still render; no real horizontal scroll on the landing page at 1440/375/320 or on Dashboard/PTW/HSE/Employees, tested by whether the document can ACTUALLY scroll rather than by comparing scrollWidth to clientWidth. No business logic, tenant isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement, workflow or data-architecture changes. Tests: 88 passing (247 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.44.0', 'date' => '2026-09-10', 'summary' => 'Depth, material and real data visualisation across the product. THE BRIGHT BLUE EVOLVED: `brand` was Tailwind stock blue -- a bright, slightly violet web blue (#2563eb) that never belonged to the same family as navy (#0F2747) and steel (#3B82B6), so side by side it read as another product accent bolted onto an industrial shell. The mid/dark stops are re-cut along the navy<->steel axis (600 is now #2166C4): still confident enough to carry a primary CTA, no longer a generic SaaS blue. The 50-200 tints are untouched -- they are near-white washes where the hue barely reads. STATCARD, the densest repeated surface in IOMS (21 pages), went from a white rectangle with a coloured edge to a real information surface: each accent now tints the whole card with a soft diagonal wash of its own hue so a red card is legible as a problem at a glance, the flat pastel icon square became a lit gradient chip with its own glow, and the card can carry an optional inline trend. A row of six of these was the single largest contributor to the "flat and white" reading -- not because any one card was wrong, but because a row of them had no material. NEW Sparkline primitive -- and it is built so the obvious fake is impossible: it renders NOTHING below two real points, never generates, smooths, interpolates or pads a series, and draws a flat series as genuinely flat rather than stretching noise into a dramatic shape. So a surface only gains a trend where the caller already had real history. Wired honestly in exactly one place where real history already existed: the Dashboard KPI Summary row now shows each category own real 12-month series -- the SAME monthlyTrend payload the chart below it already consumes, matched by the category own short_label, tinted with the category own admin-configured colour. No new query, nothing invented, and a category with no series simply gets no line. Also: PageHeader gained a steel wash and a lit top edge so it reads as a surface catching light rather than an outline drawn on the page; Card gained an OPTIONAL closed set of tones (brand/steel/navy/success/warning/danger) so a page can mark a panel as carrying a semantic without inventing its own gradient -- passing nothing renders exactly what every existing caller already got; KpiSummaryCard picked up the same lift and steel treatment. New `lift` and `glow` shadow tokens are navy-tinted rather than neutral grey, so depth reads as part of the palette instead of dirt. QA: two further real overflows found and fixed in the topbar identity cluster -- v2.43.0 truncated the user name but not the role label beneath it, which still pushed the page past the viewport at 1280. Verified with a scroll-based test (does the document ACTUALLY scroll horizontally) rather than a scrollWidth comparison, which counts the scrollbar gutter and produces false positives. Dashboard, HSE Overview, Employees, PTW and Settings verified at 320/375/390/430/1280/1440. No business logic, tenant isolation, RBAC, workflow or data-architecture changes. Tests: 88 passing (247 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.43.0', 'date' => '2026-09-09', 'summary' => 'IOMS-wide visual system, propagated through SHARED COMPONENTS rather than page-by-page styling, so the improvement reaches every module consistently instead of creating one-off treatments. THE STRUCTURAL FIX: the workspace ground was graphite-50 (#f8fafc) with two ambient washes so faint they were effectively invisible, so a white card had almost no tonal step to sit on -- that, not the components themselves, is why the product read as "a collection of white boxes on a white page". Stepping the ground to a genuinely cool graphite-100 with steel washes is what lets every existing white surface read as RAISED, and it is the reason the rest of this pass could stay restrained rather than recolouring components individually. SURFACE HIERARCHY, now an explicit three-tier ladder rather than an accident: Dashboard hero (deep navy to IOMS blue, white text -- the company-wide command surface, deliberately UNCHANGED as the reference) > department Overview band (soft steel/blue, tier two) > module page header (white panel with a navy accent rule, tier three). PageHeader was a bare h1 on the page background across 35 module pages, which is what left ordinary pages looking older than the shell around them; it is now a real raised panel. Making it navy too would have flattened the ladder and turned the product blue everywhere, which was explicitly ruled out -- it earns weight from being a real surface with a navy identity mark, not from tint. Other shared upgrades, each cascading to every caller: SectionHeader became an uppercase tracked eyebrow with a navy rule and hairline (same section language the Dashboard already used) so a page of stacked sections finally has rhythm; EmptyState became a dashed steel panel with a ringed icon chip so "nothing here yet" reads as a designed state rather than a rendering gap (64 pages); Card moved to the steel border family with a nudged shadow so it reads raised on the new ground; Table gained a tinted header band, since a data table header previously differed from its body by weight alone -- the first thing lost when scanning; Badge went to 600 weight with an inset ring so status is legible when scanning a list rather than only up close (59 pages); ModuleCard gained a navy-to-brand gradient icon chip and a hover lift so domain entry points feel like doors, not list rows. Seven pages that hand-rolled their own "No X found." text instead of using the shared EmptyState were unified. QA FINDING FIXED: the overflow sweep caught the topbar user-menu cluster pushing the page 31px past the viewport at the 1280 breakpoint -- pre-existing (this pass only recoloured the header) but real; the identity block now shrinks and truncates. Verified across Dashboard, HSE Overview, Employees, Projects, PTW, Settings and Warehouse at 320/375/390/430/1280/1440 with no real horizontal overflow (the residual 8px is the scrollbar gutter -- confirmed the document cannot actually scroll horizontally). No business logic, tenant isolation, RBAC, workflow or data-architecture changes. Tests: 88 passing (247 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.42.0', 'date' => '2026-09-08', 'summary' => 'Product/UX correction pass driven by real use of the deployed app, not another audit. PTW ACCESS 403 -- ROOT CAUSE: two authorization systems had silently diverged. User::canCreatePtw() is deliberately a UNION (canManageHse() OR an individually-granted ptw_access flag) precisely so a Field/Operations user in a NON-hse department can author permits without joining HSE -- but config/departments.php listed permits-to-work as an hse-OWNED route prefix, so RestrictDepartmentAccess 403d exactly that user at the ROUTING layer before the controller capability check could run. The prefix moved to UNIVERSAL_PREFIXES: this removes a routing-layer guess, NOT an authorization check -- the per-action gates are unchanged and a regression test pins that an UNGRANTED department user still cannot create a permit and that other HSE routes still block. DASHBOARD IA: the dashboard route used to silently return Field Home for any Department User, so an HSE-scoped account clicking "Dashboard" landed on a task-first page of HSE tiles (Create PTW / My PTW / Digital Checklist / Safety Observation) while the nav still called it Dashboard -- the one route meaning "IOMS-wide company picture" meant something different per account, and it made PTW look like the primary content of the company Dashboard. Dashboard now means Dashboard for everyone; Field Home was NOT deleted but moved to its own honest route (my-work) with its own sidebar entry, gated to Department Users. LOGIN: rebuilt as a two-panel Premium Enterprise Gateway -- a deep navy brand surface establishing what the product is, meeting a clean white working surface for credentials. Replaces five ambient decorations (blobs, pulse-glow, rotated empty square, masked grid) that carried no information on an all-white page. Every word on the navy panel is real brand copy or a live prop; no invented customer counts, uptime figures or testimonials. Collapses to a compact identity band on mobile so the form stays above the fold at 320px. VISUAL SYSTEM: the sidebar is now a deep navy rail. Earlier passes tinted it and the page behind it but both stayed in the same near-white family, so the app still read as one flat sheet. The earlier objection to a dark rail (a dark ground compresses the range available to separate active/hover/DISABLED, and disabled carries meaning here) is answered rather than ignored -- every state was re-pitched as an explicit ladder, disabled navy-500 < idle navy-300 < hover white/6 < active white/10 + white text + steel indicator, with active also carrying weight and a left bar so it never depends on hue alone. navy/steel ramps extended additively; no existing stop changed. PTW DOCUMENT: the printed permit read as a form print-out -- one hairline status chip on an all-white page and three bare underscore rules for signatures. Now an issued instrument: a navy identity band, a semantic status band whose left rule carries state as position and weight so it survives the monochrome prints these are usually issued as, bordered authorisation boxes recording who signed and when (an unapproved permit shows an empty box, never a pre-filled name), and a document-control strip making a printed copy traceable. Still A4, Dompdf-safe (table/background-colour only), verified to render. PTW WORK LOCATION: no schema change was needed -- permits_to_work already had a location column validated and rendered end to end. Project/Asset stays a real FK owned by the Management domain and location narrows the permit to an area within it; the field simply sat ABOVE Project and read as an alternative to it rather than a refinement, so it was moved beneath Project, relabeled Work Location / Area, and given a hint that it does not alter the project master. HSE MASTER DATA: "Equipment & Master Data" read as the company general equipment/inventory registry and so looked like Asset/Warehouse data misfiled under HSE. The content is genuinely HSE-owned (APAR/P3K/HT/safety cones, inspection-tracked under canManageHse()), and moving it would have put it behind canManageAssets() and cut HSE off from its own inspections -- so the fix is the label, renamed Safety Equipment & Compliance. Tests: 80 -> 88 (247 assertions). No migrations. No RBAC, tenant-isolation or entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.41.0', 'date' => '2026-09-07', 'summary' => 'Per-tenant document numbering + tenant branding assets. NUMBERING (the last known tenant-isolation gap): numbering_formats gained a tenant_id back in Milestone 3, but the COUNTER never did, so every tenant drew from one shared numbering_sequences row per module+period. Not a confidentiality breach, but each customer saw GAPS in its own document numbers wherever another customer consumed the counter, gap size weakly discloses other tenants volume, and in an HSE context a permit register with holes reads to an auditor like missing or destroyed records. Fixed with a tenant_scope column (the same always-NOT-NULL device ADR 025 established for company_scope, because every SQL engine treats each NULL in a unique index as distinct, so a nullable tenant_id in the key would not prevent duplicate rows). THE BACKFILL DELIBERATELY DOES NOT PARSE DOCUMENT NUMBERS: the earlier recommendation was to seed each tenant from MAX(issued number), which would mean reverse-engineering 29 modules editable prefix/pattern/padding formats -- fragile, and it fails once an admin customises a pattern. It is also unnecessary, because every tenant drew from the shared counter, so that counter is ALREADY >= every tenant own maximum. Seeding each tenant from the shared last_number is therefore deterministic and provably collision-free: tenant_next = shared + 1 exceeds anything that tenant holds, nothing is parsed, no counter resets to 0, and the number issued immediately after the migration is exactly the one that tenant would have received anyway -- no discontinuity at cutover. Verified on a real database: a shared counter at 42 with two tenants produced 43 for each with the platform row preserved. down() is equally careful -- it carries the MAXIMUM per-tenant value back onto the platform row before deleting tenant rows, so a rollback cannot lower the high-water mark and re-issue live numbers (verified: after a tenant advanced to 49, rollback left 49, not 42). Two tenants holding the same number string is correct and intended -- a document number is unique WITHIN a customer, like an invoice number; confirmed no module number column carries a global unique constraint. BRANDING: brand_wordmark_path and brand_icon_path have been READ since v1.5.3 with nothing able to write them; both are now on the EXISTING Settings > Company form rather than a second branding surface, and unblocked because company_settings became tenant-scoped in v2.40.0. Two real defects fixed while doing it -- ImageUploadField Remove button only cleared the PENDING file, so clicking it on a saved asset looked like it worked and silently changed nothing (a file field is simply absent when nothing is chosen, so the server could not tell no-upload from delete-this; explicit remove_* flags now carry that intent), and replaced assets were left on disk forever, leaking a file per upload. PTW: the form calls the field Penanggung Jawab Pekerjaan while every read-back surface -- the Show page, the Document view and the printed PDF -- called it PIC / Supervisor; the printed permit is the artifact an auditor actually holds, so all read surfaces now match the form. Technical identifiers (pic_employee_id, the pic relation) deliberately unchanged. Tests: 64 -> 80 (218 assertions). One migration. No functionality removed.'],
        ['version' => '2.40.0', 'date' => '2026-09-06', 'summary' => 'Full-system remediation, anchored on a P0 tenant-isolation defect in company_settings. THE ROOT CAUSE: the table was created in 2024 as a single-instance key/value store with a PLATFORM-WIDE unique(key), so it physically could not hold more than one row per key for all tenants combined; Milestone 2 added tenancy and nobody revisited it. Proven empirically, not inferred -- Tenant B READ Tenant A company name, logo, address, phone, email and brand colour, and Tenant B SAVING branding DESTROYED Tenant A because updateOrCreate([key]) matched the one shared row. It reached artifacts that LEAVE the system: DocumentEngine (every PDF letterhead), Excel export metadata, Report Center and Analytics. THE FIX: a two-tier model -- tenant_id NULL is the platform default (guests on login/landing, and any tenant without an override), tenant_id = X is that tenant override; get() resolves tenant -> platform -> caller default. A CompanySettingScope global scope fail-closes EVERY access path, not just the accessor, because five call sites deliberately bypassed it with raw Eloquent for a documented caching reason; those now use a new getUncached() that keeps the intended cache bypass without giving up tenant correctness. The cache is keyed per tier, since a bare "company_setting:{key}" key would have served one tenant a value belonging to another even after the query was scoped. MIGRATION BACKFILL is deterministic and never invented: exactly 1 tenant means ownership IS recoverable so the rows are assigned to it (fully closing the defect); 0 tenants means they are seeded platform defaults; 2+ tenants means ownership is genuinely ambiguous, so they stay as platform defaults -- preserving today exact behaviour and destroying nothing -- and a warning is emitted rather than guessing. Both branches were verified against a real database. ALSO FIXED: reports:dispatch-scheduled runs hourly with no HTTP request and therefore no resolved tenant, so TenantScope fail-closed and every scheduled report was generated EMPTY while its owner was notified it was "ready" (and titled IOMS rather than their own company) -- tenant context is now restored per schedule from the schedule own owner. Two request-input isolation gaps that survived the v2.37.0 sweep: kpi_category_id accepted another tenant taxonomy, and employee_ppe_id was a cross-tenant WRITE (storeReplacementRequest flipped the referenced row to replacement_requested and copied its owner company_id onto the new request); InCurrentTenant gained one-hop ownership resolution for tables like employee_ppe that carry no company_id of their own. Position department_id was likewise unguarded. Documentation corrected where it was actively misleading: ARCHITECTURE.md described only the pre-Milestone-2 per-company convention and never mentioned the actual isolation boundary; ROADMAP recommended building the branding UI on the broken storage model (now unblocked); ADR 025 records why numbering_sequences is still global-scope and why a naive fix would be worse. Tests: 44 -> 64 (171 assertions). No functionality removed.'],
        ['version' => '2.39.0', 'date' => '2026-09-05', 'summary' => 'Truthful zero states + repository consolidation. Browser-verified on a genuinely empty tenant: the Dashboard congratulated a brand-new customer with "Great job! No critical issues detected today," and HSE Overview rendered six KPI cards reading 0 in the healthy GREEN accent plus "Tidak ada yang terlambat saat ini" -- an all-clear asserted on a database with no employees and no records of any kind. For an HSE platform that is a safety claim nothing supports. Added TenantReadinessService (ONE memoised EXISTS query, no new table, no persisted onboarding state, no wizard -- derived live so it self-corrects), shared as a `readiness` Inertia prop for signed-in tenant users only. DashboardShell gained a single-line qualifying notice, which cascades to all nine department Overviews with zero per-controller wiring. StatCard now encodes "green is a claim": a positive semantic accent downgrades to neutral when no data could have produced it, while amber/red pass through untouched so a real warning is never silently muted. Propagated the honest pattern HSE Man-Hour already used ("--" plus an explicit unavailable reason) rather than inventing a second language for it. Also documented -- with a passing characterisation test -- a confirmed cross-tenant defect in company_settings (see ROADMAP "BLOCKED"); no schema change made. Brand integrity: the forbidden long-form product name removed from CompanySettingSeeder (a fresh install seeded it, and it then reached PDFs, Excel exports and the app shell), README, CHANGELOG and CLAUDE.md.'],
        ['version' => '2.38.0', 'date' => '2026-09-04', 'summary' => 'SaaS foundation (Master Audit P1). SecureDocumentController: authenticated, tenant-checked document delivery driven by a whitelist registry keyed by type + record id, with the path read from the database and never from the URL -- it streams from the new private disk OR the legacy public disk, so it ships with ZERO file migration. Subscription seat limits were previously only ever DISPLAYED, never enforced -- user creation now checks entitlement inside a locking transaction, reusing the exact shape already proven for the PTW quota. EmployeeSelector added as a shared platform primitive: seven controllers were preloading an entire employee directory into the page payload just to render a <select>, unusable at the 1,500-3,000 headcount IOMS targets; it is async, debounced, out-of-order-response guarded, department-grouped and id-hydrating, backed by a tenant-scoped EmployeeLookupController capped at 50 rows. Deliberately NOT built on Combobox (that one is free-text-over-preloaded-strings) and deliberately NOT solved by making pic_name free text, which would have destroyed the ability to answer "which permits was this person responsible for?" -- exactly what an HSE audit asks.'],
        ['version' => '2.37.0', 'date' => '2026-09-03', 'summary' => 'Security + testability (Master Audit P0). Three CONFIRMED cross-tenant defects fixed after auditing all 172 route-model-bound controller methods (163 were already correctly guarded): ProjectController::update never invoked its policy, allowing a cross-tenant WRITE; PpeController and TaskController allowed cross-tenant READS of employee PII, the latter handling a nullable tasks.company_id. Added the reusable InCurrentTenant validation rule, replacing unscoped `exists:` across 17 FormRequests so a submitted foreign id can no longer reference another tenant record; it memoises the tenant company set per rule instance so wildcard rules do not issue one lookup per row. Automated testing was impossible before this release and is now unblocked: phpunit.xml simply did not exist, and six migrations used MySQL-only syntax (MODIFY COLUMN, multi-table UPDATE ... JOIN, information_schema) that no other driver could run -- each is now driver-aware with the MySQL path preserved byte-identical, so production behaviour is unchanged and all 148 migrations provision on SQLite. Also fixed a real PTW timezone defect: the form seeded datetime-local inputs from an ISO/UTC string, the likely root cause of the reported "UI 4:37 PM vs PDF 08:37" mismatch.'],
        ['version' => '2.36.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Visual System 2.0: a genuine, visible transformation rather than another token-only pass. Added two new design tokens completing the directional palette (navy, steel) to the existing brand/graphite/semantic system -- no parallel color system. Dashboard\'s hero banner rebuilt from a barely-tinted white box into a real deep-navy-to-IOMS-blue "Command Center" surface (white text, an explicit "IOMS · Industrial Operations Platform" eyebrow, filter controls restyled for the dark surface). DashboardShell -- the one shared component every department Overview (HSE, Warehouse, Logistics, Procurement, Project Management, Assets, Maintenance, Quality Control) already composes through -- rebuilt from plain PageHeader into a soft steel/blue "domain summary surface," deliberately lighter than the Dashboard hero so Dashboard-vs-Overview stays visually distinct while both read as part of the same system; every existing caller gets this automatically, no page-level changes needed. Sidebar gained a subtle top-to-bottom tint (was flat white); TopBar gained a faint blue-tinted backdrop. PeriodFilter gained an optional triggerClassName so its selects can be restyled for a dark surface without duplicating the component. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, PTW workflow, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.35.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Visual System + Information Architecture Refinement: fixed a real reported mobile bottom-nav bug -- visibleNav\'s own "Dashboard" entry was duplicating the hardcoded "Home" tab (both pointing at the same route), and grouped items (e.g. HSE\'s "Permit & Work Safety") were skipped entirely, so PTW could never reach the bar while unrelated top-level items like Waste Management filled the slots instead. Rebuilt the candidate logic to dedupe Dashboard, surface each group\'s first real child (generic, not HSE-specific), and deprioritize Waste Management out of the primary slots per this pass\'s explicit direction -- Waste remains fully reachable via Overview/sidebar/More, only demoted from this one high-frequency bar. Renamed the pinned first slot "Home" -> "Dashboard" (there is no separate Home concept). AuthenticatedLayout\'s background gained a second, bottom-of-viewport tint layer addressing the repeated "too white" production finding -- the v2.29.0 tint only reached the top-left corner of a tall scrolled page. Most of this phase\'s scope (Dashboard vs Overview distinction, HSE Inventory, Digital Checklist, PTW Access, Waste language/workflow, landing page positioning, password visibility) was audited and confirmed already correctly addressed in prior phases (ae83d48 and earlier) -- not re-touched without a confirmed gap. No migrations, no RBAC/tenant-isolation/PTW-workflow/entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.34.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Post-Deployment Product Gap pass: audit-first review of six named production concerns found each was either already fully built (just undiscoverable) or genuinely absent. HSE Inventory (APAR/P3K/HT/safety cones) already existed in full under "HSE Master Data" -- relabeled to "Equipment & Master Data" with an explicit subtitle instead of being rebuilt. "Digital Checklist" (a Dashboard/Field Home quick-action label) and the sidebar\'s "Inspection" item were the same hse-inspections feature under two different names -- sidebar relabeled to match. PTW Access management existed only inside Settings > Users with zero sidebar entry point -- added one (same queryParams.tab pattern Administration items already use, no new authorization gate). Legal & Compliance confirmed genuinely absent (broad codebase search for license/regulatory/statutory/compliance found nothing) -- reported as missing, not fabricated. Waste Management\'s remaining English explanatory text (Dashboard/Form/Index/Show subtitles, an EmptyState, a CardDescription) naturalized to Indonesian per the established hybrid-language rule; Waste module/field names stayed English. Confirmed the "HSE-heavy Dashboard" concern in production actually describes Field/Home.jsx (a distinct, intentionally task-first page for the narrow is_department_user account type), not the actual Dashboard/Index.jsx (already cross-domain and already restructured in v2.29.0) -- documented, not conflated. No migrations, no RBAC/tenant-isolation/PTW-workflow/entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.33.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Phase 4 (Operational Documents + Waste + Backup/Security Audit): audit-first pass across Document/Waste/Backup/Security/Public Website. Real finding -- Waste Management\'s full generate-to-vendor-handover lifecycle (WasteRecord/WasteMovement/WasteMovementDocument) already existed with a real state machine; only a formal printable manifest was missing, added as waste-movements.pdf reusing the exact same DocumentEngine/PdfGeneratorService pipeline PTW/Material Request already use (no migration, no new PDF engine). Security audit found and fixed three real gaps: login/forgot-password/reset-password routes had no rate limiting (added Laravel\'s built-in throttle:6,1), WasteMovementController\'s document upload had no mimes: whitelist (added, matching every sibling upload validator), and .env.example still shipped the stale full product-name branding (corrected to "IOMS" so a fresh deployment starts correct). Extracted a new shared PasswordInput component and applied it to 7 password fields across Settings and Platform > Tenants that had no show/hide toggle (Login/Reset Password already had one since v2.27.0). Backup architecture audited and found to be a manual, on-demand, same-server-only mysqldump feature with no automation or off-site copy -- documented as a finding, not silently implemented as a false "solved." Public website positioning re-confirmed intact, not rebuilt. No migrations, no RBAC/tenant-isolation/PTW-workflow/entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.32.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Interior UI Completion Phase 3B (People, Operations, KPI): Employees Index and Man-Hour/Work Orders desktop tables switched their avatar/identity cells to the shared PersonChip component (brand-tinted, consistent with Employee Profile). Leave, Daily Reports, and Work Orders each gained a mobile identity-first card list (previously relied entirely on horizontal table scroll on narrow viewports), an unboxed filter toolbar, and consolidated desktop columns from 6-7 equal-weight columns down to 3-5 grouped cells -- no data dropped. Man-Hour\'s two summary boxes switched to StatCard (same already-real total_hours/record_count values). KPI Records gained real color-coded category quick-filter chips (each category\'s own admin-configured color, not a fabricated count). No target/trend/achievement percentage was added anywhere in KPI -- confirmed again that no such field exists in the data model. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, leave/workflow, or Man-Hour calculation changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.31.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Interior UI Transformation Phase 3 (People, Operations, Management/KPI, Settings, Micro-interactions): Employee Profile\'s identity block now sits on its own soft-blue tinted header band (brand-tinted avatar, name/code/status) separated from Work Information below, instead of one flat card. KPI Records gained a real primary summary card (total records, semantically accented when a category filter is active) before its table -- no fabricated target/trend, since no target field exists anywhere in the KPI data model (confirmed, not built around). Projects Index cards gained a status-accent bar (planned/ongoing/completed/cancelled), extending StatCard\'s own accent convention into Operations. Settings\' 16-tab wall regrouped into 5 visually separated clusters (Company, Master Data, People & Access, Workflow & Documents, System) -- same tabs, same RBAC gates, grouping only. Reusable micro-interactions added: Button gained real press feedback (motion-safe active:scale), CollapsibleSection\'s expand/collapse switched from an instant pop to the same smooth CSS-grid technique already proven on the sidebar\'s own sub-menus, Settings\' tab active-state gained a transition. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, or PTW/A4 document changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.30.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Interior UI Transformation Phase 2 (Domain Experience): actual domain-page redesigns, not another shared-component-only pass. Reports gained a real visual treatment -- PageHeader, KpiSummaryCard-based key summary (same icon/color-driven cards Dashboard already uses, same KpiCategory data, no fabrication), and department table headers switched from a solid color band to a tinted section-header style. Global Dashboard\'s HeroSummary now shifts its own surface tone (soft blue "all clear" vs soft amber "attention") instead of only a small footer strip. Procurement Dashboard\'s 8 stat cards regrouped into Pipeline / Delivery & Completion / Vendors & Value with eyebrow labels, communicating actual workflow position. Warehouse\'s "Barang Stok Menipis" card gets a real amber-tinted header when low-stock items exist. Logistics\' existing Material Flow pipeline visualization was deliberately preserved (already good, per this pass\'s own instruction) -- only its surrounding surface treatment changed, with the current bottleneck stage (computed from the same real per-stage counts already on screen) getting a stronger visual accent. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, or PTW/A4 document changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.29.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Authenticated UI Visual Transformation: shared-component-first pass, not a page-by-page audit. StatCard gained a real accent color-bar, hover lift, and an opt-in `hint` line -- cascades automatically into every domain dashboard already using it (HR, HSE, Logistics, Maintenance, PPE, Procurement, Project Management, Quality Control, Warehouses, Assets, Corrective Actions, Work Orders, the Global Dashboard). ModuleTabNav\'s active tab now gets a tinted surface instead of a bare underline, plus an optional real-data `meta` count line -- wired live into PPE\'s Employee/Replacement Due/Replacement Requests tabs from each page\'s own already-loaded paginator/array data, never fabricated. AuthenticatedLayout gained a fixed, very-low-opacity blue-tinted background layer (white Cards/Sidebar/TopBar still read as the brighter surface on top). Dashboard gained explicit "Company Snapshot" / "Needs Attention" section labels separating primary state from operational-attention KPIs. MobileBottomNav\'s active tab gained a top indicator bar + tinted icon pill. EmptyState\'s icon chip switched from plain neutral gray to the app\'s own soft-blue surface. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, or PTW/A4 document changes -- the A4 density work from 66addf6 is untouched.'],
        ['version' => '2.28.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Product Experience Transformation, Deliverable B (PTW A4 Document): PTW Document.jsx and pdf/permit-to-work.blade.php redensified for a real A4 print target -- title/section/field/signature spacing roughly halved, Permit Type and PTW Number now share one compact row instead of stacking, keeping every section, field, and data point unchanged (numbered 01-05 information architecture from v2.20.0 untouched). Deliverable A (philosophy correction): the one Enterprise-as-"custom" line on the public Pricing section reworded to reflect IOMS as a standardized SaaS product -- Enterprise as the most complete tier (broader access, capacity, reporting), not bespoke development; is_custom flag, PricingService, and entitlement logic untouched. Broader interior-UI domains (Dashboard/HSE/Warehouse/etc.) re-audited and confirmed already consistent with the shared-component visual language established across v2.15.0-v2.27.0 -- no further change made where none was warranted.'],
        ['version' => '2.27.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Public Website & Auth Visual Transformation: redesigned Hero with a platform visualization (central IOMS hub + 8 real domain nodes, lg:-only for safe mobile fallback), a distinct English "Built for Industrial Operations" industry statement, and a white -> light-blue -> soft-blue ambient system with subtle motion-safe animation (2 new Tailwind keyframes, no new dependency). Redesigned pricing cards with visual hierarchy (no invented "Most Popular" claim) and plan framing text. Login gained a show/hide password toggle (also added to Reset Password) and the same visual identity as the public site. Public/authenticated app boundary, RBAC, business logic, and pricing data untouched.'],
        ['version' => '2.26.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Final Copy Consistency Check: naturalized the Dashboard hero subtitle, Material Requests subtitle, and four Warehouse/Item Master page subtitles to Indonesian. Actually inspected (not blindly modified) the PHP flash-message layer -- confirmed the existing ~176 messages are already uniformly clear, professional English; fixed the one genuine inconsistency found (5 messages with a redundant "...successfully." suffix). Logistics Dashboard and navigation labels re-confirmed already correct, left unchanged. Pure copy pass -- no layout, business-logic, or database change. This closes the IOMS UI/UX + copy consistency work.'],
        ['version' => '2.25.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Global UX & Copywriting Polish: naturalized 18 confirm() dialogs from English "Remove this X?" to consistent Indonesian "Hapus X ini?" across Daily Reports/Employees/HSE Inspection/Leave/Man-Hour/Milestones/PPE/Report Center/RFQ/Settings; added Indonesian explanatory copy to Login/Forgot Password/Reset Password (labels/buttons stay English); Settings Subscription tab copy naturalized; public website hero tagline corrected from a drifted "Industrial Operations Management Platform" back to "Industrial Operations Platform", subheadline naturalized to Indonesian. Pure copy pass -- no layout, component, or business-logic change. PHP-side controller flash messages (176 call sites) not systematically reviewed -- flagged as a follow-up, not silently skipped.'],
        ['version' => '2.24.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Complete Product UI/UX Transformation, closing the remaining gaps: Purchase Requisition (Purchasing) and KPI Records (Management) got the same identity-first table + mobile card treatment as prior passes; Reports gained a client-side "key summary" strip (no fabricated data) read before the detailed matrices; Logistics Dashboard and Report Center reviewed and found already good (Logistics already has a genuine pipeline visualization, not just cards). Presentation only -- no business logic, RBAC, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.23.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Complete Product UI/UX Transformation, continued: extended the identity-first list pattern (established on PTW/Incidents) to one representative page per remaining domain -- Safety Observation (HSE), Employees (People/HR), Man-Hour (Operations), Goods Receipt (Warehouse/Procurement) -- each gaining a mobile card list where none existed, consolidated desktop columns, and an unboxed filter bar. Public website hero/platform copy, Dashboard hierarchy, and Settings/Subscription were reviewed and found already adequate -- left unchanged. Presentation only -- no business logic, RBAC, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.22.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Complete Product UI/UX Transformation pass: new fixed mobile bottom navigation bar (Home + up to 3 authorized items + More, reusing the exact same RBAC/department-filtered nav array and drawer the sidebar already uses -- no second permission system); "IOMS" restored as the primary product name in the browser tab title, app config fallback, and public site footer (was drifting toward the full "Integrated Operations Management System" expansion, and this environment\'s own .env had a stale pre-rebrand "Shipyard Management System" title); Incidents list got the same identity-first table consolidation PTW Index introduced last pass. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, SaaS entitlement, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.21.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Final Visual Polish pass (P0 scope): PTW Index desktop table consolidated from 9 equal-weight columns to 6 identity-first cells (Permit = number+type, Project/Location, Requester/PIC, each a two-line grouped unit) -- no data dropped, real visual hierarchy instead. Filter bar unboxed from its Card. Presentation only. Broader multi-module polish (Dashboard/HSE/PPE/etc.) intentionally left for a follow-up pass given this pass\'s own P0-first priority and proportionate scope.'],
        ['version' => '2.20.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Experience & Visual Polish pass: redesigned PTW Document/PDF around a numbered information architecture (01 Work Information -> 05 Supporting Documents) with a dominant document-title block and PIC/Workforce shown via a new reusable PersonChip avatar component instead of plain text; PTW Show page gained a more prominent identity/status header; PTW Create form gained numbered step badges. Presentation only -- no schema, workflow, RBAC, tenant isolation, SaaS entitlement, or quota changes. Same PdfGeneratorService/DocumentEngine pipeline, no new PDF engine.'],
        ['version' => '2.19.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Access Management Correction pass: fixed settings.users.ptw-access being gated role:super_admin only at the route level -- STRICTER than SettingsController::updatePtwAccess()\'s own canManageHse() check, meaning HSE could never actually reach it despite the controller already allowing it. Moved to its own role:super_admin,hse route group. Settings > Users tab now opens for HSE too, but split into two independent cards -- User Management (create/edit/delete/role changes) stays Super-Admin-only; Field & PTW Access (toggle + quota) opens to HSE. Requester/PIC/Workforce/quota logic re-audited and confirmed already correct, no changes needed. No HSE Dashboard, Field Home, PTW workflow, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.18.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Public Website / Landing Page Foundation: `/` is now a genuinely public route (previously redirected straight to /login for anyone not signed in) showing a real product website -- hero, platform capabilities, PTW-to-HSE workflow story, Field/HSE/People/Data sections, industries, FAQ, and a Pricing section driven live from Package data (never a hardcoded price). New PublicLayout (separate from AuthenticatedLayout, no sidebar). No customer names/logos/counts/testimonials invented anywhere. Authenticated users hitting `/` are still redirected into the app exactly as before. No RBAC, tenant isolation, PTW workflow, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.17.1', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Field Workflow Verification & Correction pass: fixed the max_users(10)/max_ptw_users(15) contradiction on Starter by raising max_users to 15, and PlatformController::validatePlan() now server-enforces max_ptw_users <= max_users for any future Plan edit; PTW Index (HSE workspace) now shows Project/PIC/Workforce headcount on desktop and mobile, via N+1-free eager loading. Re-audited Requester/PIC/Workforce/security -- all confirmed correct, no changes needed there. No HSE Dashboard, Field Home scope, PTW state machine, pricing, billing, or checkout changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.17.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Field Workflow Foundation + Controlled PTW Access: new per-user "PTW Access" grant (users.ptw_access) lets specific non-HSE Field/Operations users create PTWs, capped by a new package-level PTW user quota (packages.max_ptw_users, server-enforced with race-safe locking); Field Home\'s Create PTW tile now reflects this instead of HSE-role-only; PTW gained optional PIC/Supervisor and Workforce (real Employee references, tenant-scoped) shown consistently on Show/Document/PDF. Requester was already correctly server-derived -- confirmed, not changed. No HSE Dashboard, PTW state machine, RBAC, tenant isolation, or subscription state machine changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.16.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Global Mobile UX Hardening pass: fixed ModuleTabNav (PPE\'s tab bar) causing page-level horizontal scroll on mobile -- the root cause of a reported PPE screenshot regression -- by containing the scroll to the tab row itself; fixed two PPE list rows (Employees, Replacement Due) whose fixed-width columns exceeded narrow viewports; sidebar drawer now closes explicitly on mobile nav-item tap. Audited broadly (tabs, tables, dialogs, grids, fixed widths) -- most tables were already safely self-scrolling via the shared Table component\'s own wrapper, so no table changes were needed. No business logic, HSE Dashboard, Field Home, PTW workflow, RBAC, tenant isolation, subscription, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.15.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'Product UI/UX Finalization: fixed the dialog component app-wide so tall forms no longer clip off-screen on mobile (max-h + internal scroll + safe margin, previously only one page had opted in); PageHeader now stacks title-above-actions on mobile instead of squeezing both onto one row; added a warning Badge variant so "Overdue"/"High priority" read distinctly from "Rejected"/"Critical"; Incident list converted to the proven PTW mobile-card pattern. Audit-first, shared-components-first pass -- no business logic, dashboard widgets, sidebar structure, or Field Home scope changed.'],
        ['version' => '2.14.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'SaaS Productization / Pricing Foundation: Package confirmed as the canonical Plan entity (currency/trial_days/is_public/is_custom added, no new plans table); new PricingService is the single source of formatted plan pricing (never hardcoded in a component); new tenant-facing Plans comparison page; trial-days-to-trial_ends_at wiring; upgrade/downgrade and billing-ready architecture documented for a later phase. No checkout, payment gateway, or final pricing decision -- explicitly out of scope.'],
        ['version' => '2.13.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'SaaS Phase 1 -- Subscription Architecture & Entitlement Enforcement: server-side per-workspace entitlement enforcement enabled by default (previously built, never wired on); a tenant with no grant rows is now safely treated as fully allowed instead of fully denied, so this cannot lock out a pre-existing tenant; new `tenants:sync-grants` command additively tops up a partially-granted tenant to its Package baseline; entitlement-denied messages now in Bahasa Indonesia. No new table, no payment/billing/pricing/checkout work (explicitly out of scope for this phase).'],
        ['version' => '2.12.0', 'date' => '2026-09-07', 'summary' => 'Product Finalization pass: fixed three real cross-tenant leaks found by a fresh security audit (Material Request full IDOR across 9 controller methods, PPE Replacement Request view/PDF IDOR, KpiRecordController default-state full leak), fixed a hardcoded-branding PDF footer, added two missing delete confirmations, improved the Employees empty state. No database change, no new route.'],
        ['version' => '2.11.0', 'date' => '2026-09-06', 'summary' => 'Field/Foreman Experience pass, Phase 3E-3H: Digital Checklist reworked into one-tap OK/Not OK/N/A cards (no more sideways scrolling), Safety Observation/JSA/HIRADC mobile grid fixes, Incident investigation Recommendations now visible read-only, a real "New LOTO" quick action added (previously had zero entry point). No database change, no new route.'],
        ['version' => '2.10.0', 'date' => '2026-09-05', 'summary' => 'PTW Document Polish pass (Phase 3D): fixed a real PDF/browser-document parity gap (rejection reason was missing from the PDF), HIRADC/JSA now show title/job_title not just the reference number, print output made dark-mode-safe with proper A4 page setup and break-inside protection. No database change, no new route.'],
        ['version' => '2.9.0', 'date' => '2026-09-04', 'summary' => 'Field/Foreman Experience pass (Phase 3C -- My PTW): new requester-scoped, card-based My PTW view (permits-to-work.mine) with real status filter counts and inline Resubmit for rejected permits; Field Home\'s My PTW tile now links here with live pending/active counts. No new table, no new role, no database change.'],
        ['version' => '2.8.0', 'date' => '2026-09-03', 'summary' => 'PTW Mobile / Task-First pass (Phase 3B): mobile card list for PTW Index (status always visible, no horizontal scroll), a real "resubmit" action for rejected permits (the state machine already allowed it, the UI never exposed it), rejection reason surfaced on the Show page, remaining mobile grid fixes.'],
        ['version' => '2.7.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Field/Foreman Experience pass (Phase 3A): the universal dashboard route now branches to a new task-first Field Home page for Department Users -- large action tiles (Create PTW/My PTW/Checklist/Observation/Incident/My Tasks), reusing existing routes and WorkCenterService counts, no new RBAC, no database change.'],
        ['version' => '2.6.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'PTW Document View pass: new in-browser, printable PTW document presentation (company header, work info, hazards/HIRADC/JSA, gas test, authorization, signatures), reached via a new primary "View PTW Document" action on the PTW detail page; Download PDF unchanged, reusing the existing PdfGeneratorService.'],
        ['version' => '2.5.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Field HSE Experience pass (Phase 2): CAPA Open/Overdue/In Progress/Closed summary + Overdue filter, Global Search gained HIRADC/JSA, PPE Master vs Operations made visually distinct, mobile-responsive fixes across HSE Inspection/Incident/PPE, natural-Indonesian empty states with primary actions.'],
        ['version' => '2.4.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'PTW UX + Field Operations pass (Phase 1): PTW PDF document generation, Create form progressive disclosure, Submit now reaches Pending Approval in one step, Reject requires a reason, mobile/responsive fixes across the module.'],
        ['version' => '2.3.0', 'date' => '2026-08-29', 'summary' => 'HSE Operations pass: new Waste Container Inventory (physical drum/IBC/jumbo-bag stock, separate from waste material records), Man-Hour 500 fix re-verified, PPE Master Edit audited and confirmed correct, Quick Actions gained a Waste entry.'],
        ['version' => '2.2.0', 'date' => '2026-08-25', 'summary' => 'IOMS OS Ecosystem pass: Man-Hour 500 root-caused and fixed (ambiguous SQL column), Quick Actions and Work Center Action Center extended across every department, Global Search extended (PPE/CAPA/PTW/Assets/Vendors) and its cross-tenant scoping gap fixed, a second cross-tenant leak fixed in Work Center approvals/PPE-alerts, sidebar navigation language reverted to English.'],
        ['version' => '2.1.0', 'date' => '2026-08-25', 'summary' => 'HSE Starter package made genuinely operational: Man-Hour opened to HSE (entitlement-dependency-rule fix), Package-to-Workspace/Module grant mapping wired into tenant creation, PPE Dashboard "+" action fix, optional per-workspace entitlement enforcement (off by default).'],
        ['version' => '2.0.0', 'date' => '2026-08-16', 'summary' => 'Milestone 2: Tenancy Foundation, Platform Super Admin, Package/Subscription structure, RBAC (spatie/laravel-permission), DB-driven Module and Workspace catalogs, Platform Super Admin console.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.10', 'date' => '2026-08-11', 'summary' => 'Material Request complete workflow (HasWorkflow trait); RBAC options evaluated (Spatie Permission recommended, docs/ADR/006); new Warehouse role.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.9', 'date' => '2026-08-09', 'summary' => 'Universal Approval Engine and Activity Timeline viewer -- verified ActivityLog already existed before building anything new.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.8', 'date' => '2026-08-08', 'summary' => 'Employee Import from Excel; Report Export architecture prepared; two severe runtime bugs found and fixed via verification, not assumption.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.7', 'date' => '2026-08-06', 'summary' => 'ModuleTabNav reusable navigation; several desktop density passes; Material Request and PPE Replacement Request MVPs; reusable PdfGeneratorService.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.6', 'date' => '2026-08-03', 'summary' => 'PPE employee-centric restructure; navigation redesign; Browser QA stabilization (white flash, Select bug sweep).'],
        ['version' => '1.6.5', 'date' => '2026-08-02', 'summary' => 'About Dialog rebuilt from scratch; sidebar branding hierarchy fixed; six shared foundation components.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.4', 'date' => '2026-08-01', 'summary' => 'Universal Task Engine foundation; QA stabilization pass across Dashboard, Sidebar, Dark Mode.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.3', 'date' => '2026-07-28', 'summary' => 'Dashboard/Sidebar/TopNav/Theme/Company Branding usability pass; real Global Search; Forgot/Reset Password.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.2', 'date' => '2026-07-27', 'summary' => 'Root-cause fix for watermark clipping; exact 240px/70px/16px/24px branding specs; searchable Combobox.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.1', 'date' => '2026-07-26', 'summary' => 'Fixed a real PHP parse error; Dashboard Today\'s Summary; Top Department Workload; About fields expanded.'],
        ['version' => '1.6.0', 'date' => '2026-07-25', 'summary' => 'Enterprise UI refresh: centralized config/ioms.php, About dialog scrolling fixed, branding sizing pass.'],
